export const DOCS: readonly DocEntry[] = [
  { slug: "member-cards", section: "features", title: "Member cards", summary: "Each member gets an ID card they can show on a phone or print.", body: "# Member cards\n\nEach member gets an ID card they can show on a phone or print.\n\n## What it does\n\n- One live card per member and plan, with a QR code that opens a public check page.\n- A card designer lets an organisation set colours, fonts and layout. Designs can differ by role.\n- Managers save drafts. Only the Owner approves one, and members' cards keep the approved design until then.\n- Card numbers follow a pattern you set, with a running sequence. Renewals keep the number.\n- Cards are drawn on demand as PDF or PNG, so the two always match. They are never stored.\n- Scanning a card as signed-in staff of that organisation shows the member record, inside their unit scope. Everyone else sees only whether the card is valid.\n- Bulk downloads give a ZIP of PDFs or one combined PDF.\n\n## Who can use it\n\n- Issuing, reissuing and emailing cards needs `cards.manage`. Editing designs needs `cards.design`. Approving a design needs `cards.approve`, the Owner's alone.\n\n## Turning it on or off\n\nCards are issued when a membership is first activated. The check page works whether or not the member portal is on.\n\n## Screenshots\n\n![Member cards in the light theme](/screens/member-cards-light.webp)\n\n![Member cards in the dark theme](/screens/member-cards-dark.webp)\n\n## API\n\nThere is no public REST API yet. The source is on [GitHub](https://github.com/SpaceWorks-Labs/SpaceWorks-Organization)." },
  { slug: "member-portal", section: "features", title: "Member portal", summary: "Members manage their own membership from /account.", body: "# Member portal\n\nMembers manage their own membership from `/account`.\n\n## What it does\n\n- Members see their memberships, history, payments and receipts.\n- They can renew, withdraw a renewal, leave, and ask for a refund.\n- Their records are found by their verified email address.\n- Where the organisation allows it, they upload their own card photo.\n- They can download their data and ask for their details to be erased. Erasure anonymises the person, and money records are kept.\n\n## Who can use it\n\nAny member with a verified account. Staff review erasure requests with the `erasure.decide` permission.\n\n## Turning it on or off\n\n- Member accounts are a switch on the organisation. Off, members get emails that link to the public page or to a signed pay link instead.\n- Member photo upload is a separate switch, off by default.\n\n## Screenshots\n\n![Member portal in the light theme](/screens/member-portal-light.webp)\n\n![Member portal in the dark theme](/screens/member-portal-dark.webp)\n\n## API\n\nThere is no public REST API yet. The source is on [GitHub](https://github.com/SpaceWorks-Labs/SpaceWorks-Organization)." },
  { slug: "notifications", section: "features", title: "Notifications", summary: "Members and the team hear about what matters, by email and chat.", body: "# Notifications\n\nMembers and the team hear about what matters, by email and chat.\n\n## What it does\n\n- Member emails, such as notices, reminders, receipts and cards, go out in the organisation's name.\n- An organisation can use its own SMTP, Amazon SES, Resend or Postmark account, or fall back to the platform's. A test email checks the settings before they are saved, and addresses that bounce or complain are not emailed again.\n- Organisations can change the wording of member emails with plain-text templates and placeholders.\n- Team chat alerts go to Slack, Discord, Mattermost, Telegram, WhatsApp or a signed webhook. Alerts cover new applications, payments, refund and erasure requests, and a monthly summary.\n- Messages are queued with the change that causes them, so a failed change never sends anything.\n\n## Who can use it\n\nEmail and chat settings are managed by Owners and Managers.\n\n## Turning it on or off\n\nEach chat channel picks its own alerts and can be switched off. The organisation's own email settings can be turned on or off, and a test email checks them.\n\n## Screenshots\n\n![Notifications in the light theme](/screens/notifications-light.webp)\n\n![Notifications in the dark theme](/screens/notifications-dark.webp)\n\n## API\n\nThere is no public REST API yet. The source is on [GitHub](https://github.com/SpaceWorks-Labs/SpaceWorks-Organization)." },
  { slug: "organisations", section: "features", title: "Organisations", summary: "Each organisation is its own tenant, with its own members, plans, team and settings.", body: "# Organisations\n\nEach organisation is its own tenant, with its own members, plans, team and settings.\n\n## What it does\n\n- Every organisation is separate. Its members, money and settings are never visible to another organisation.\n- A person can sign up an organisation, and the platform super admin reviews it before it goes live. The super admin can also create one directly.\n- An organisation can be public or unlisted. Public organisations appear in the directory at `/organisations` and take applications from anyone. Unlisted ones are reached only by their link.\n- The super admin can set limits on members and team size for each organisation.\n\n## Who can use it\n\n- Anyone with an account can apply to create an organisation.\n- The platform super admin approves, declines or creates organisations.\n- Inside an organisation, settings need the `org.settings` permission. Deleting an organisation and transferring ownership belong to the Owner alone.\n\n## Turning it on or off\n\n- The super admin can switch new account sign-ups on or off for the whole platform.\n- Each organisation chooses whether it is listed in the public directory.\n\n## Screenshots\n\n![Organisations in the light theme](/screens/organisations-light.webp)\n\n![Organisations in the dark theme](/screens/organisations-dark.webp)\n\n## API\n\nThere is no public REST API yet. The source is on [GitHub](https://github.com/SpaceWorks-Labs/SpaceWorks-Organization)." },
  { slug: "payments", section: "features", title: "Payments", summary: "Collect membership fees online or offline, and keep an honest record of every rupee.", body: "# Payments\n\nCollect membership fees online or offline, and keep an honest record of every rupee.\n\n## What it does\n\n- Each organisation picks one online gateway, Razorpay or Stripe, or none. Offline is always available.\n- Offline collection covers UPI QR, bank transfer, cash and cheque. A manager records it, or the member submits \"I've paid\" with a reference that a manager then confirms.\n- Units can have their own payment instructions, and a collection sheet records one transaction per member.\n- Members who cannot sign in pay through a signed pay link in their email.\n- Amounts are whole paise. Gateway keys are stored encrypted and never sent back to the browser.\n\n## Who can use it\n\n- Viewing payments needs `payments.view`. Recording and confirming them needs `payments.manage`.\n- Gateway and tax settings need `payments.settings`.\n\n## Turning it on or off\n\nGateways are switched on by saving keys under Settings, Payments and tax. Without the platform's `PAYMENT_SECRETS_KEY` the gateway section stays locked and only offline payment works.\n\n## Screenshots\n\n![Payments in the light theme](/screens/payments-light.webp)\n\n![Payments in the dark theme](/screens/payments-dark.webp)\n\n## API\n\nThere is no public REST API yet. The source is on [GitHub](https://github.com/SpaceWorks-Labs/SpaceWorks-Organization)." },
  { slug: "plans-and-join-forms", section: "features", title: "Plans and join forms", summary: "Plans describe what people can join, and join forms collect what you need to know.", body: "# Plans and join forms\n\nPlans describe what people can join, and join forms collect what you need to know.\n\n## What it does\n\n- A plan has a name, a fee, a period and optional joining and rejoining fees.\n- A plan can need approval. Applications then wait in a queue until staff approve or decline them.\n- A plan can be limited to chosen units.\n- Join forms are built from questions on each plan: built-in fields, custom questions, choose-several answers, sections and conditional questions that appear only when an earlier answer calls for them.\n- A plan can ask for a photo when someone joins.\n- One answer is kept for each question, even when the person holds several plans.\n- A public join page and unit join links (`?unit=CODE`) bring people in.\n\n## Who can use it\n\n- Creating and editing plans and join forms needs `plans.manage`.\n- Approving and managing members needs `members.manage`. Staff limited to a unit can approve only inside that unit.\n\n## Turning it on or off\n\nA plan can be set to accept applications or not, and to need approval or not. Each question is shown or hidden on the plan it belongs to.\n\n## Screenshots\n\n![Plans and join forms in the light theme](/screens/plans-and-join-forms-light.webp)\n\n![Plans and join forms in the dark theme](/screens/plans-and-join-forms-dark.webp)\n\n## API\n\nThere is no public REST API yet. The source is on [GitHub](https://github.com/SpaceWorks-Labs/SpaceWorks-Organization)." },
  { slug: "public-page", section: "features", title: "Public page", summary: "Every organisation can have a public page at /o/<slug> that tells people who you are and how to join.", body: "# Public page\n\nEvery organisation can have a public page at `/o/<slug>` that tells people who you are and how to join.\n\n## What it does\n\n- A sections editor builds the page from blocks of text, links, images and plans.\n- Text is written in a simple Markdown subset and is shown as plain text, never as raw HTML. Links must be web, email or phone links.\n- Images must be your own uploads.\n- Changes are kept as a draft until you publish. You can discard changes or unpublish.\n- The page can use the organisation's own accent colour, checked for readable contrast.\n\n## Who can use it\n\nEditing the page needs `org.settings`.\n\n## Turning it on or off\n\nPublish and Unpublish control whether the page is visible. Whether the organisation is listed in the directory is a separate setting.\n\n## Screenshots\n\n![Public page in the light theme](/screens/public-page-light.webp)\n\n![Public page in the dark theme](/screens/public-page-dark.webp)\n\n## API\n\nThere is no public REST API yet. The source is on [GitHub](https://github.com/SpaceWorks-Labs/SpaceWorks-Organization)." },
  { slug: "receipts-and-refunds", section: "features", title: "Receipts and refunds", summary: "Every payment gets a proper document, and every correction leaves a trail.", body: "# Receipts and refunds\n\nEvery payment gets a proper document, and every correction leaves a trail.\n\n## What it does\n\n- Paid payments produce GST receipts or tax invoices, or simple receipts where GST does not apply.\n- Numbers run without gaps for each organisation in each financial year, April to March in the organisation's time zone.\n- A paid payment is never edited or deleted. A mistake is reversed with a credit note.\n- Members can ask for a refund. The organisation sets a refund policy, and staff approve or decline each request.\n- Approved refunds go back through the gateway where there is one, and pending gateway refunds are followed up automatically.\n- Other receipts and expenses can be kept in an accounts ledger, voided once and never deleted.\n\n## Who can use it\n\n- Viewing money needs `payments.view`. Issuing credit notes and handling refunds needs `payments.manage`.\n- The accounts ledger needs `accounts.manage`. GSTR-1 tables need `reports.gstr1`, which is for the whole organisation only.\n\n## Turning it on or off\n\nThe tax mode is chosen under Settings, Payments and tax. The refund policy is set by the organisation.\n\n## Screenshots\n\n![Receipts and refunds in the light theme](/screens/receipts-and-refunds-light.webp)\n\n![Receipts and refunds in the dark theme](/screens/receipts-and-refunds-dark.webp)\n\n## API\n\nThere is no public REST API yet. The source is on [GitHub](https://github.com/SpaceWorks-Labs/SpaceWorks-Organization)." },
  { slug: "renewals", section: "features", title: "Renewals", summary: "Memberships run in terms, and renewals keep a member's standing and card without a gap.", body: "# Renewals\n\nMemberships run in terms, and renewals keep a member's standing and card without a gap.\n\n## What it does\n\n- Each membership is a term with a start, an end and, where the plan allows, a grace period.\n- A background job moves terms through active, in grace and lapsed, and sends renewal reminders.\n- Members renew from their account, early or late. A renewal keeps the same card and number.\n- Staff can send reminders in bulk from the renewals worklist, one manual reminder per membership per day.\n- A lapsed member can rejoin, and a joining or rejoining fee applies if the plan sets one.\n\n## Who can use it\n\n- Staff need `members.manage` to renew, reinstate or send reminders.\n- The renewals tab in reports needs `reports.membership`.\n\n## Turning it on or off\n\nRenewal reminders and grace are set on each plan. The member portal switch decides whether members renew from their account or through a payment link in the email.\n\n## Screenshots\n\n![Renewals in the light theme](/screens/renewals-light.webp)\n\n![Renewals in the dark theme](/screens/renewals-dark.webp)\n\n## API\n\nThere is no public REST API yet. The source is on [GitHub](https://github.com/SpaceWorks-Labs/SpaceWorks-Organization)." },
  { slug: "reports-and-analytics", section: "features", title: "Reports and analytics", summary: "Understand membership and money, with every figure counted the same way each time.", body: "# Reports and analytics\n\nUnderstand membership and money, with every figure counted the same way each time.\n\n## What it does\n\n- Organisation reports have tabs for overview, units, members, renewals, applications, money, tax and receipts, and team activity.\n- Filters cover period, plan and unit.\n- The units tab compares the units one level below with drill-down.\n- Reports export as CSV, and the overview prints as a PDF snapshot.\n- Platform analytics for the super admin compare organisations and show trends. They contain counts and totals only, never a member's name, email or phone.\n- All figures are counted in house from the application's own data. No outside provider is involved.\n- Web visits (Reports, Web visits) shows views of the organisation's public pages by day and by page, with a CSV. Visits are counted by Space Works itself without cookies or personal details. The platform overview shows platform and per-organisation totals.\n\n## Who can use it\n\n- Membership reports need `reports.membership`. Money and tax need `reports.finance`. Team activity needs `audit.view`.\n- Platform analytics are for the super admin only.\n- Web visits needs `reports.membership` and is open to staff limited to a unit, because it is public-page information rather than member data.\n\n## Turning it on or off\n\nReports are always on. Staff limited to a unit see only their own units.\n\n## Screenshots\n\n![Reports and analytics in the light theme](/screens/reports-and-analytics-light.webp)\n\n![Reports and analytics in the dark theme](/screens/reports-and-analytics-dark.webp)\n\n## API\n\nThere is no public REST API yet. The source is on [GitHub](https://github.com/SpaceWorks-Labs/SpaceWorks-Organization)." },
  { slug: "self-hosting", section: "features", title: "Self-hosting", summary: "You can run Space Works Organization on your own server. It is open source under the AGPL.", body: "# Self-hosting\n\nYou can run Space Works Organization on your own server. It is open source under the AGPL.\n\n## What it does\n\n- A one-line installer sets up a single server with Docker. It checks the host, asks a few questions and starts the whole stack.\n- Three layouts are supported: a single server (VPS), a cloud setup with managed services, and a hybrid with the website on Netlify.\n- Backups cover the database and stored files, with an optional off-site copy, and can be restored from the menu.\n- Updates and rollbacks are a single command.\n- The platform super admin account is created on the server only.\n\n## Who can use it\n\nThe person who runs the server. Inside the product, only the platform super admin sees platform settings.\n\n## Turning it on or off\n\nOptional parts, such as the bundled storage or the Netlify frontend, are chosen when you install. See the operator guides for the steps.\n\n## Screenshots\n\n![Self-hosting in the light theme](/screens/self-hosting-light.webp)\n\n![Self-hosting in the dark theme](/screens/self-hosting-dark.webp)\n\n## API\n\nThere is no public REST API yet. The source is on [GitHub](https://github.com/SpaceWorks-Labs/SpaceWorks-Organization)." },
  { slug: "sign-in", section: "features", title: "Sign-in", summary: "How people sign in: email and password, Google, Apple, GitHub, any OpenID Connect provider, SAML 2.0, and optional Supabase or Firebase accounts.", body: "# Sign-in\n\nHow people sign in: email and password, Google, Apple, GitHub, any OpenID Connect provider, SAML 2.0, and optional Supabase or Firebase accounts.\n\n## What it does\n\n- People sign in with an email and password. Every other way is off until the person running the platform switches it on.\n- The sign-in page shows the buttons for the methods that are on first, then an \"or\" line, then the email and password fields.\n- Supabase and Firebase can be used as identity providers too. They open their own sign-in, and this site accepts the result only when the provider confirms the email address. They sign in the accounts they created here: an email that already has an account must keep signing in the way it does now. Keep \"Confirm email\" on in the Supabase project.\n- OpenID Connect and SAML providers are added by the platform's super admin, any number of them. Like Supabase and Firebase, they never take over an existing account by email: if that email already has an account, the sign-in is refused and the person signs in the usual way.\n- Google, Apple and GitHub can join an existing account by its verified email. A sign-in with no verified email, such as a phone number alone, is refused.\n- New browsers are remembered, and a first sign-in from a new device sends an alert. Accounts without an authenticator get an emailed code from a browser they haven't used, when the platform can send email.\n- Several wrong passwords for one email address slow that address down for a while (15 seconds, doubling up to 15 minutes), whether or not an account exists.\n- Staff still need an authenticator before the dashboard opens, whichever method they used.\n- Super admins always sign in with an email and password set on the server. They have no passkeys.\n- Passkeys sign people in with a fingerprint, face or screen lock. Add one under Security in your account while signed in; the sign-in page then shows \"Sign in with a passkey\" and suggests passkeys in the email field. Staff with an authenticator still enter its code after a passkey. You can rename and remove passkeys, but not the last way you have to sign in.\n- Without an email service, \"Forgot password?\" is hidden and an administrator resets the password.\n- Under Security in your account, \"Signed-in devices\" lists the browsers and devices signed in to you, with the way each signed in, a rough place and when it was last used. Sign any of them out, or sign out everywhere. Ending a device signs it out on its next page load.\n- \"Sign-in methods\" shows every way you can sign in. Add Google, GitHub, a company single sign-on, SAML, Supabase or Firebase while you are signed in: it links that account to yours (it never links by email address, and never takes an account that already belongs to someone else). Remove any you no longer want, as long as another way to sign in remains. Super admins sign in with a password only.\n- With an authenticator app on, you can replace your recovery codes under Security by entering a current code from the app. The old codes stop working.\n\n## Who can use it\n\nEveryone signs in. The platform super admin chooses which methods are offered, under Auth in the super-admin area. The person running the server can set the list at install time or with the admin command.\n\n## Methods\n\n| Method | Default | Set up by |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Email and password | On | Nothing to set up |\n| Google | Off | Client id and secret, in Super admin settings or the server environment |\n| Apple | Off | Services ID, team ID, key ID and key |\n| GitHub | Off | Client id and secret |\n| OpenID Connect | Off | Add providers under Auth: issuer, client id, secret, scopes |\n| SAML 2.0 | Off | Add providers under Auth: entity id, sign-on address, certificate |\n| Supabase, Firebase | Off | Public project values in the server environment |\n| Passkeys | Off | Nothing to set up; people add their own. They work only on the site address they were created for |\n| Phone code | Off | An SMS provider (Twilio or MSG91) under Auth → SMS, or in the server environment |\n\n## Phone codes\n\nWith phone codes on and an SMS provider set up, people can add a phone number on their account page (Security): they type it with its country code, get a six-digit code by text, and confirm it. After that, \"Sign in with a phone code\" on the sign-in page texts a code to that number. The code works once, for 10 minutes, with five tries. It never creates an account, a number belongs to one account, and super admins sign in with a password only. If your account has an authenticator app, you enter its code as well. You can't remove your phone if it is your only way to sign in.\n\nThe sign-in page answers the same way for every number (\"If this number belongs to an account, we've sent a code\"), and asking for too many codes for one number, one country or one address is refused with a wait. It works even when no email service is set up.\n\n## Turning it on or off\n\nEach method has a switch. A method works only when its switch is on and it has been set up, so switching one on never makes an unconfigured method work, and switching one off keeps its credentials. For OpenID Connect and SAML, each provider also has its own Enabled switch. A method that is off is hidden everywhere and refuses sign-ins. If the server fixes the list, the switches show as set by the server and can't be changed in the app. With email and password off, the sign-in page hides the password form and sign-up, and super admins reach theirs from the Super admin sign-in link.\n\n## Screenshots\n\n![Sign-in in the light theme](/screens/sign-in-light.webp)\n\n![Sign-in in the dark theme](/screens/sign-in-dark.webp)\n\n## API\n\nThere is no public REST API yet. The source is on [GitHub](https://github.com/SpaceWorks-Labs/SpaceWorks-Organization)." },
  { slug: "staff-and-roles", section: "features", title: "Staff and roles", summary: "Run the organisation with a team, each person holding only the permissions they need.", body: "# Staff and roles\n\nRun the organisation with a team, each person holding only the permissions they need.\n\n## What it does\n\n- Each organisation defines its own roles from a fixed list of permissions, for example `members.view`, `payments.manage`, `reports.finance` and `cards.design`.\n- Four starter roles come ready: Owner, Manager, Treasurer and Viewer. The Owner holds every permission and cannot be edited or given by invite.\n- A role can be held for the whole organisation or for one unit.\n- People join the team by a single-use invite link that carries a role and, optionally, a unit.\n- You can give someone only a role that grants strictly less than you hold, inside your own reach.\n- Every member of staff must set up an authenticator app before the dashboard opens.\n- A platform super admin with an authenticator can act in any organisation as its owner. This is never shown as a team seat and is recorded in the audit log.\n\n## Who can use it\n\n- Managing the team needs `team.manage`. Creating and editing roles needs `roles.manage`.\n- Transferring ownership is the Owner's alone.\n\n## Turning it on or off\n\nRoles and the team are always on. Members never need an authenticator, only staff do.\n\n## Screenshots\n\n![Staff and roles in the light theme](/screens/staff-and-roles-light.webp)\n\n![Staff and roles in the dark theme](/screens/staff-and-roles-dark.webp)\n\n## API\n\nThere is no public REST API yet. The source is on [GitHub](https://github.com/SpaceWorks-Labs/SpaceWorks-Organization)." },
  { slug: "units", section: "features", title: "Units", summary: "Units let an organisation mirror how it is really arranged, such as areas and branches.", body: "# Units\n\nUnits let an organisation mirror how it is really arranged, such as areas and branches.\n\n## What it does\n\n- The organisation names its own levels, up to four, for example \"Area\" then \"Unit\". Nothing is fixed by the product.\n- It builds a tree of units. Each unit has a short code of 1 to 6 letters or digits, unique inside the organisation.\n- The code is used in join links (`?unit=CODE`) so people join the right unit, and in card numbers.\n- A unit can move only to a parent on the same level. Its subtree moves with it. Levels and card numbers never change.\n- Plans can be limited to some units, and a limited plan is also offered to everything below those units.\n\n## Who can use it\n\n- Building and changing the unit tree needs the `structure.manage` permission.\n- Staff given a role for one unit see and act only inside that unit and the units below it. They can read, but not change, organisation-wide screens.\n\n## Turning it on or off\n\nUnits are optional. An organisation that does not add any simply has no unit level. Names and levels are set under Settings, in the structure section.\n\n## Screenshots\n\n![Units in the light theme](/screens/units-light.webp)\n\n![Units in the dark theme](/screens/units-dark.webp)\n\n## API\n\nThere is no public REST API yet. The source is on [GitHub](https://github.com/SpaceWorks-Labs/SpaceWorks-Organization)." },
  { slug: "web-analytics", section: "features", title: "Web analytics", summary: "Optional outside analytics, available in 0.9.0.", body: "# Web analytics\n\nOptional outside analytics, available in 0.9.0.\n\n## What it does\n\n- Outside providers, such as Plausible, Umami, Google Analytics 4, PostHog and Mixpanel, are all off by default.\n- With no provider set up, no script loads, no request is made and nothing is stored.\n- Scripts load only after a visitor agrees in the consent banner, and withdrawing consent stops them at once.\n- Only page route templates are sent, never free text, query strings, names or email addresses.\n- The platform operator can set up providers for the whole site, and each organisation can set up its own for its public pages.\n- An organisation's own providers run only on its own public pages and join forms, only for signed-out visitors who agree. The platform's providers do not run there, so a page never runs two page-analytics providers.\n- Space Works also counts visits itself, always, with no cookie and no personal data: just how many views each kind of public page had each day. These counts need no consent and appear under Reports, Web visits.\n\n## Who can use it\n\nThe platform super admin for the whole site, and the organisation's owner for its own public pages (settings, Web analytics). A super admin can also set up any organisation's. Visit counts are read by anyone who can read membership reports.\n\n## Turning it on or off\n\nSwitch a provider on by saving its public identifier in settings. Remove it to switch it off. Outside hosts must first be approved by the operator, and Mixpanel is set by the operator only. An organisation's public page lists the providers it uses and links to Privacy preferences.\n\n## Screenshots\n\n![Web analytics in the light theme](/screens/web-analytics-light.webp)\n\n![Web analytics in the dark theme](/screens/web-analytics-dark.webp)\n\n## API\n\nThere is no public REST API yet. The source is on [GitHub](https://github.com/SpaceWorks-Labs/SpaceWorks-Organization)." },
  { slug: "architecture", section: "operators", title: "Architecture", summary: "One Next.js 16 application (App Router, React Server Components, Server Actions) with Prisma 6 on PostgreSQL 16. Organisations are tenants: every tenant row carries…", body: "# Architecture\n\n## Application\n\nOne Next.js 16 application (App Router, React Server Components, Server Actions) with Prisma 6 on\nPostgreSQL 16. Organisations are tenants: every tenant row carries `organisationId`, every query is scoped\nthrough the organisation resolved for the request, and cross-tenant access returns 404. There is no database\nrow-level security; isolation is enforced in the application.\n\n| Area | Where |\n|---|---|\n| Pages and Server Actions | `app/`, `components/`, `lib/views/` |\n| Domain logic (memberships, money, receipts) | `lib/membership/`, `lib/money/` |\n| Sign-in | Auth.js v5 (`lib/auth.ts`), email + password, authenticator MFA, Google, GitHub, Apple |\n| Email | Nodemailer behind a database outbox (`lib/outbox.ts`) |\n| Files | S3-compatible storage (`lib/storage/`), served through `/api/uploads/...` |\n| Rate limits | Redis (`lib/rateLimit.ts`) |\n| Read cache | Redis (`lib/cache.ts`), see \"Read cache\" below |\n| Start-up validation | `lib/env.ts`, called from `instrumentation.ts` |\n| Background jobs | BullMQ on Redis (`lib/queue/`), see \"Background jobs\" below |\n\n### Background jobs\n\nBackground work runs on BullMQ in two processes built from the backend image:\n\n- **`beat`** (`scripts/queue/beat.ts`, `dist/beat.js`) upserts the repeatable schedules from\n  `SCHEDULED_JOBS` in `lib/queue/registry.ts` once per start, and removes schedules whose entry is gone.\n- **`worker`** (`scripts/queue/worker.ts`, `dist/worker.js`) runs every queue: registry entries by name, and\n  outbox rows one id at a time.\n\n| Queue | Carries | Default concurrency |\n|---|---|---|\n| `outbox` | Email and chat outbox rows, by id, and the `outbox-relay` entry | 5 |\n| `scheduled` | Every other periodic registry entry | 2 |\n\n| Registry entry | Every | Work |\n|---|---|---|\n| `outbox-relay` | minute | Queue due outbox rows (and stale claims) by id |\n| `membership-sweep` | minute | Term statuses, renewal reminders, the report rollup |\n| `payment-reconcile`, `refund-reconcile` | 5 minutes | Ask Razorpay or Stripe about open gateway payments and refunds |\n| `monthly-digest` | hour | The monthly summary, once per organisation and month |\n| `expired-backfill`, `storage-cleanup` | minute | One-off clean-ups in small chunks; idle once done |\n| `operational-retention` | minute | Expired tokens, sessions and invites; old outbox rows |\n| `worker-heartbeat` | minute | The worker's own heartbeat row |\n\n- New periodic work is an entry in the registry and nowhere else. A failing entry is retried by BullMQ and\n  never stops the others.\n- Redis carries only row ids and registry names, never email content, links or personal data. PostgreSQL is\n  the durable outbox and owns delivery retry timing (`docs/NOTIFICATIONS.md`).\n- Jobs retry eight times with exponential backoff; the failed set is the dead-letter store (30 days, at most\n  5,000). Stored error messages are stripped of URLs, bodies and credentials.\n- Both processes validate the environment at start and answer `GET /health` on `JOBS_HEALTH_PORT` (3460),\n  healthy only while their Redis heartbeat is fresh.\n- Hosts that cannot keep a beat process call `POST /api/internal/jobs` (the registry) and\n  `POST /api/internal/outbox` once a minute with `Authorization: Bearer $CRON_SECRET`; a worker still runs the\n  jobs. Time-bucket job ids make repeated calls in the same minute harmless.\n- Local development without Redis may set `JOBS_INPROCESS=true`: the web process then runs every periodic job\n  from a one-minute timer (`runOutboxScheduler` in `lib/outboxWorker.ts`). Production never does this.\n\n`npm run build:jobs` (`scripts/build-jobs.mjs`, esbuild) bundles both entry points into `dist/`.\n\n### Read cache\n\n`lib/cache.ts` is a small read-through cache on the rate limiter's Redis client (`REDIS_URL`; no setting of its\nown). `cached(key, ttlSeconds, load)` returns the stored value or runs `load` and stores the result. It is\nfail-open: with no Redis, a Redis error or a value that won't serialise, the loader's result is returned, so\npages work the same without it. Values are JSON; Dates are tagged and revived, Maps and Sets are never stored.\n\nKeys are built only by `orgCacheKey` (`swo:cache:v1:org:<organisationId>:<scope>[:<extra>]`),\n`analyticsCacheKey` (the same, with the viewer's unit scope, a unit id or `org`, always next) and\n`platformCacheKey` (`swo:cache:v1:platform:<scope>`). Permission checks run before `cached`, and a cached value\nnever holds per-viewer data that isn't in its key. `invalidateOrg(organisationId, ...scopes)` drops every key\nof those scopes for one organisation.\n\n| What | TTL | Key | Dropped when |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Platform analytics (page and CSV export) | 5 min | `platform:analytics` | TTL only |\n| One organisation's super-admin analytics | 5 min | `org:<id>:analytics:org:platform` | its rollup rewrites days |\n| Dashboard home charts (membership, money) | 5 min | `org:<id>:analytics:<unit>:home-members` / `home-money` | its rollup rewrites days |\n| Units league | 5 min | `org:<id>:analytics:<unit>:units:<plan>:<from>-<to>:<money/members>` | its rollup rewrites days |\n| Public page plans, published page, units | 10 min | `org:<id>:public-page` | plans created, changed or archived; page published or unpublished |\n\nThe organisation behind `/o/<slug>` (status, name, logo, branding) is read live, so suspending or rebranding\nshows at once. Money state, permissions, member records, cards, `/verify`, pay pages and write paths are\nnever cached.\n\n## Images and roles\n\nTwo images come from the one `Dockerfile` and are published to GHCR by `.github/workflows/release.yml`:\n\n| Image | Target | Roles (`scripts/docker-entrypoint.sh --role …`) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `spaceworks-organization-backend` | `runner` | `backend` (web app and API), `migrate` (`prisma migrate deploy`), `worker` (`node dist/worker.js`), `beat` (`node dist/beat.js`), `ops` (operator checks) |\n| `spaceworks-organization-frontend` | `frontend` | `frontend`: the website alone for split hosting (the `frontend/` workspace) |\n\nRelease images are identical for every installation. The public origins compiled into Next.js are placeholders\n(`spaceworks-organization-origin.invalid`, `spaceworks-organization-backend.invalid`) that the entrypoint\nreplaces at start from `NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_URL` and `NEXT_PUBLIC_BACKEND_ORIGIN`. The Server Actions key used to\nbuild is a throwaway; web roles refuse to start without the installation's own key. PostgreSQL, Redis, Silo and\nCaddy run from their own upstream images.\n\n## Deployment topologies\n\n```\nBundled (docker-compose.prod.yml)                       Cloud (docker-compose.cloud.yml)\n\n internet ── caddy :80/:443 ──┐  edge network            platform TLS ── backend :8080\n                              ▼                                          │\n            backend   worker   beat                  worker, beat ───┘\n               │  (TLS, private CA)    data network          migrate (one-shot)\n   ┌───────────┼───────────┐  (internal, no egress)              │\n   db        redis       minio ◄─ createbuckets             managed PostgreSQL, Redis (TLS),\n   ▲ migrate (owner role)  ▲ backup-storage                 S3/R2 bucket, SMTP relay\n   └ backup (pg_dump)\n```\n\n- **Bundled:** everything on one server through `scripts/spaceworks-compose.sh bundled`, with overlays for\n  automatic HTTPS (`docker/compose.tls.yml`), an existing proxy (`docker/compose.proxy.yml`), an external\n  bucket (`docker/compose.external-storage.yml`) and source builds (`docker/compose.build.yml`).\n  PostgreSQL, Redis and Silo accept TLS only, with certificates from the install's private CA\n  (`deploy/certificates.sh`). Migrations run as the owner role `sworg`; the app runs as `sworg_app`, which can\n  read and write data but cannot change the schema or roles.\n- **Cloud:** the backend image's roles on any Docker host (`scripts/spaceworks-compose.sh cloud`) or on Render\n  (`render.yaml`), with managed services.\n- **Split:** the website on Netlify (or the frontend image) and the backend in either topology above; the\n  website reaches the backend only through the signed gateway (`frontend/catalog.json`,\n  `FRONTEND_GATEWAY_SECRET`). See [DEPLOYMENT_NETLIFY.md](DEPLOYMENT_NETLIFY.md).\n\n## Releases\n\n`VERSION` selects the series. Every push to `main` runs the tests, publishes both images (amd64 and arm64)\ntagged `<VERSION>-main.<run>.<sha>` and `sha-<sha>`, creates a GitHub release with that tag, promotes the\nimages to `latest`, `main` and the series, prunes superseded image versions (keeping the previous release for\nrollback) and, with `SWO_DEPLOY=true`, deploys through a self-hosted runner. Pushes to `dev` publish images\ntagged `dev` and `<VERSION>-dev.<run>.<sha>` only. The installer and updater accept only published,\nnon-prerelease `main` releases." },
  { slug: "configuration", section: "operators", title: "Configuration reference", summary: "Every environment variable the app, its images and the deployment scripts read. Templates: .env.example (development) and .env.production.example (the bundled install's .env and…", body: "# Configuration reference\n\nEvery environment variable the app, its images and the deployment scripts read. Templates:\n[`.env.example`](../.env.example) (development) and [`.env.production.example`](../.env.production.example)\n(the bundled install's `.env` and the cloud `cloud.env`). The installer generates every secret for you.\n\nIn production the app, worker and beat validate their settings at start (`lib/env.ts`) and refuse to run with a missing secret,\na plain-text database or Redis connection, or mismatched public origins.\n\n## Application\n\n| Variable | Required | Description |\n|---|---|---|\n| `NODE_ENV` | production | `production` turns on the start-up validation, secure cookies and HSTS. |\n| `DATABASE_URL` | yes | Runtime PostgreSQL URL. Production needs `sslmode=require`, `verify-ca` or `verify-full`; a transaction pooler on `:6543` needs `pgbouncer=true`. Keep at least 2 connections in the pool (`connection_limit` ≥ 2): the worker's storage clean-up holds one while it works through another. The bundled stack uses the least-privilege role `sworg_app`. |\n| `DIRECT_URL` | no | Direct or session connection for `prisma migrate` (a role that owns the schema). Defaults to `DATABASE_URL`. Same TLS rule. |\n| `REDIS_URL` | production | Rate limits, the read cache and the BullMQ job queues (`docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`). Production requires `rediss://`. |\n| `AUTH_SECRET` | yes | Auth.js secret, 32+ characters. |\n| `AUTH_URL` | production | The public origin. Must equal `NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_URL`. |\n| `NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_URL` | production | The canonical origin for links in receipts, cards and email. Release images get it at start (placeholder replacement); your own builds compile it in. |\n| `ALLOW_PLAIN_HTTP` | no | `1` allows an `http://` origin, for a LAN or trial install on plain HTTP. Never on the internet. |\n| `NEXT_PUBLIC_HOSTED_BY` | no | Footer credit \"Hosted by …\". |\n| `LANDING_ENABLED` | no | `true` (default) shows the landing page at `/`. `false` makes `/` follow the data (product rules §12.2): the one public organisation, the organisations list when there are several, or sign-in when there are none. Runtime value; set it on the website (and the backend in split hosting). |\n| `WEB_TELEMETRY_ALLOWED_ORIGINS` | no | Comma-separated bare `https://` origins the operator approves for optional outside web analytics: collectors you host (Plausible, Umami, PostHog) and each vendor's script and collection hosts (see `docs/SECURITY.md`). Settings can only point at an origin listed here, and the Content Security Policy widens for these origins on public pages only. Runtime value, never `NEXT_PUBLIC_`; set it on the backend and the website. Empty keeps the strict default. |\n| `WEB_TELEMETRY_MIXPANEL_TOKEN` | no | Public Mixpanel project token. Off without it. While set, Mixpanel replaces the page-analytics provider (after consent) and its hosts are added to the policy on public pages. Never committed. |\n| `WEB_TELEMETRY_MIXPANEL_API_HOST`, `WEB_TELEMETRY_MIXPANEL_SDK_HOST` | no | Bare `https://` origins; default `https://api.mixpanel.com` (EU projects: `https://api-eu.mixpanel.com`) and `https://cdn.mxpnl.com`. The SDK host must not be this site's own origin. |\n| `ALLOWED_ACTION_ORIGINS` | no | Extra hostnames allowed to call Server Actions (a CDN in front). Build time only; release images refuse it. |\n| `TRUSTED_PROXY_IP_HEADER` | production | The client-IP header your edge overwrites: `x-real-ip` (bundled Caddy), `cf-connecting-ip` (Cloudflare) or `x-forwarded-for`. |\n| `TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS` | no | With `x-forwarded-for`, how many trusted proxies append to it (default 1). |\n| `CRON_SECRET` | production | Bearer secret for `POST /api/internal/jobs` and `POST /api/internal/outbox`, the fallback for hosts without a beat process. |\n| `OUTBOX_RETENTION_DAYS` | no | Days to keep delivered outbox rows (30). |\n| `HEALTHCHECK_SECRET` | production | Bearer secret for `GET /api/health/db`. `/api/health` is open liveness. |\n| `NEXT_SERVER_ACTIONS_ENCRYPTION_KEY` | production | Base64 of a 16, 24 or 32-byte key, stable across every container. Release images refuse to start without it. |\n| `MFA_ENCRYPTION_KEY` | production | Base64 of 32 bytes; encrypts authenticator seeds. Never change it once in use. |\n| `PAYMENT_SECRETS_KEY` | production | Base64 of 32 bytes; AES-GCM key for Razorpay and Stripe secrets at rest. Generated by the installer; back it up. |\n| `SUPER_ADMIN_EMAIL` | yes | The first super admin. The installer creates it with the password you give; super admins are created, revoked and given passwords only on the server with `bash scripts/admin.sh`. An account with this address from before 0.7 is kept as super admin on upgrade. |\n| `PWNED_PASSWORDS_CHECK` | no | `1` (default) refuses new passwords found in known data breaches, using Have I Been Pwned with k-anonymity (only a 5-character SHA-1 prefix leaves the server). `0` turns it off, for installs without internet access. |\n| `EMAIL_FROM` | yes | Sender, `Name <address@verified-domain>`. Super admin → Settings overrides the email settings. |\n| `EMAIL_PROVIDER` | no | `smtp` (default), `ses`, `resend` or `postmark`. Email stays off until the chosen provider is fully set up. Super admin → Settings overrides it. |\n| `SMTP_HOST`, `SMTP_PORT`, `SMTP_SECURE` | with `smtp` | Relay host, port (587, or 465 with `SMTP_SECURE=true`). |\n| `SMTP_USER`, `SMTP_PASSWORD` | with `smtp` | Relay login. |\n| `AWS_SES_REGION`, `AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID`, `AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY` | with `ses` | Amazon SES v2 region (`AWS_REGION` is used when empty) and an access key allowed to send. `AWS_SESSION_TOKEN` is optional for temporary keys; instance-role lookup is not supported. |\n| `SES_TOPIC_ARN` | no | The one SNS topic SES bounce and complaint reports may come from. Without it SES reports are refused. |\n| `RESEND_API_KEY` | with `resend` | Resend API key. |\n| `POSTMARK_SERVER_TOKEN`, `POSTMARK_MESSAGE_STREAM` | with `postmark` | Server token and message stream (`outbound`). |\n| `EMAIL_WEBHOOK_SECRET` | no | Resend's webhook signing secret (`whsec_…`) or the password Postmark's webhook sends as basic auth. |\n| `SMS_PROVIDER` | no | `twilio` or `msg91`: how phone sign-in codes are texted. Off until its fields are set; Super admin → Auth wins over these. |\n| `TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID`, `TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN` | with `twilio` | Account SID and auth token. The token also verifies delivery reports. |\n| `TWILIO_API_KEY_SID`, `TWILIO_API_KEY_SECRET` | no | An API key instead of the auth token for sending (delivery reports then need the auth token too). |\n| `TWILIO_FROM`, `TWILIO_MESSAGING_SERVICE_SID` | with `twilio` | The sending number with its country code, or a messaging service SID (used instead when both are set). |\n| `MSG91_AUTH_KEY`, `MSG91_TEMPLATE_ID` | with `msg91` | Auth key and the approved (DLT) Flow template, which takes the variables `otp` and `brand`. |\n| `MSG91_SENDER_ID` | no | The registered sender id, when the template does not carry it. |\n| `SMS_WEBHOOK_SECRET` | no | The token MSG91's delivery report address carries (`?token=`). Twilio reports are checked by their signature. |\n| `SMS_DAILY_COUNTRY_CAP` | no | The most phone codes asked for numbers of one country calling code in a day, across the install. Default 500. |\n| `SMTP_SERVICE` | no | A Nodemailer well-known service instead of a host. |\n| `SMTP_ALLOW_UNAUTHENTICATED` | no | `1` only for a private relay without authentication. |\n| `S3_BUCKET`, `S3_REGION` | production | The private bucket. |\n| `S3_ENDPOINT` | no | S3-compatible endpoint; HTTPS in production. |\n| `S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID`, `S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY` | no | Both or neither (an IAM role). |\n| `S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE` | no | `1` for Silo, MinIO and R2. |\n| `AUTH_GOOGLE_ID`, `AUTH_GOOGLE_SECRET` | no | Google sign-in fallback (`GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID`/`GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET` also read). Super admin → Settings overrides. |\n| `AUTH_GITHUB_ID`, `AUTH_GITHUB_SECRET` | no | GitHub sign-in fallback. Only a verified primary email can sign in. |\n| `LOGIN_METHODS` | no | The deployment's choice of sign-in methods: a comma list of `password`, `google`, `apple`, `github`, `oidc`, `saml`, `supabase`, `firebase` and `passkey` and `phone`. When set it wins over the Super admin switches (shown there as set by the server, read-only); a method still needs its own configuration (for `oidc` and `saml`, at least one enabled provider). Unset leaves the switches to Super admin, where only password is on by default. A provider configured only here (`AUTH_GOOGLE_ID` and the like) stays off until it is switched on. The installer sets it when you pick extra options. Super admins always sign in with a password. |\n| `AUTH_SUPABASE_URL`, `AUTH_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY`, `AUTH_SUPABASE_PROVIDERS` | no | Supabase Auth bridge: project URL, publishable (anon) key (public values) and a comma list of `email` and the OAuth providers the project enables (default `email`). The project must use asymmetric JWT signing keys; the legacy shared HS256 secret is refused. |\n| `AUTH_FIREBASE_API_KEY`, `AUTH_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN`, `AUTH_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID`, `AUTH_FIREBASE_APP_ID`, `AUTH_FIREBASE_PROVIDERS` | no | Firebase Auth bridge: public web app values and a comma list from `google.com`, `microsoft.com`, `apple.com`, `github.com`, `facebook.com`, `twitter.com`, `yahoo.com`, `emailLink`, `password` (default `google.com`). `AUTH_FIREBASE_APP_ID` is optional. |\n| `AUTH_APPLE_ID`, `AUTH_APPLE_TEAM_ID`, `AUTH_APPLE_KEY_ID`, `AUTH_APPLE_PRIVATE_KEY` | no | Apple sign-in fallback: services ID, team ID, key ID and the `.p8` key with newlines as `\\n`. |\n| `BACKUP_PROVIDER`, `BACKUP_LAST_SUCCESS_AT`, `BACKUP_RETENTION_DAYS` | no | Backup posture shown in Super admin → Operations. `BACKUP_RETENTION_DAYS` also sets how long scheduled dumps stay (14 in the bundled stack). |\n| `MIGRATE_ON_START` | no | `1` makes the backend role apply migrations before starting (Render). |\n| `PORT`, `HOSTNAME` | no | Listening port and address inside the container (3000, `0.0.0.0`). |\n| `NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS` | no | Extra CA for Node; the bundled stack points it at its private CA. |\n| `NEXT_DEV_ALLOWED_ORIGINS` | no | Development only: extra hosts allowed to load dev assets. |\n\n### Background jobs (worker and beat)\n\nThe `worker` and `beat` roles (`npm run build:jobs`, then `npm run worker` and `npm run beat`) run every\nbackground job on BullMQ over `REDIS_URL` (`docs/ARCHITECTURE.md`).\n\n| Variable | Default | Description |\n|---|---|---|\n| `JOBS_QUEUE_PREFIX` | `swo:jobs` | Prefix for the BullMQ keys in Redis. Lowercase letters, digits, `:`, `_` and `-`, up to 40 characters. Give two installations sharing one Redis different prefixes. |\n| `JOBS_HEALTH_PORT` | `3460` | Port of the worker's and beat's `GET /health`. |\n| `QUEUE_OUTBOX_CONCURRENCY`, `QUEUE_SCHEDULED_CONCURRENCY` | 5, 2 | Jobs one worker runs at once per queue, 1 to 64. |\n| `JOBS_INPROCESS` | `false` | Development only, and only without `REDIS_URL`: `true` runs every periodic job (outbox delivery, membership sweep, retention…) from a one-minute timer in the web process. Ignored in production. |\n\n### Object storage\n\nEvery stored file (logos, page images, card artwork, member photos) goes to one private bucket through one\nclient (`lib/storage/client.ts`). Member photos sit under `organisations/<org>/members/<member>/`, so erasing a\nmember deletes that prefix. Card files are never stored: every card, single or bulk, is drawn when it is asked for.\n\n| Store | `S3_ENDPOINT` | `S3_REGION` | `S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE` |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Silo (MinIO) on a VPS | `https://minio:9000` (set by the bundled stack) | any, `us-east-1` by default | `1` |\n| Cloudflare R2 | `https://<account-id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com` | `auto` | `1` |\n| Amazon S3 | empty | the bucket's region | `0` |\n\nThe access key needs to read, write, delete and list objects in the bucket (R2: an API token with Object Read &\nWrite, limited to the bucket). On a self-managed bucket with versioning (MinIO, S3), also grant\n`s3:ListBucketVersions` on the bucket and `s3:DeleteObjectVersion` on its objects: erasing a member or a photo\ndeletes every stored version, not just the current one. The bundled Silo policy (`deploy/storage-policy.json`)\nhas both. Without them, only current objects are deleted, the earlier versions stay, and the server logs it.\nThe application sends checksums only where S3 requires them, which R2 and Silo both accept.\n\n### Split hosting (website on Netlify)\n\n| Variable | Where | Description |\n|---|---|---|\n| `DEPLOYMENT_MODE` | backend | `backend` serves only the API and Server Actions; `monolith` (default) serves everything. |\n| `FRONTEND_GATEWAY_SECRET` | both | 64 hex characters shared by the website and the backend. |\n| `BACKEND_ORIGIN`, `NEXT_PUBLIC_BACKEND_ORIGIN` | website | The backend's HTTPS origin. The frontend image gets it at start. |\n| `WEB_TELEMETRY_ALLOWED_ORIGINS`, `WEB_TELEMETRY_MIXPANEL_TOKEN`, `WEB_TELEMETRY_MIXPANEL_API_HOST`, `WEB_TELEMETRY_MIXPANEL_SDK_HOST` | both | Optional outside web analytics. The website's proxy builds the Content Security Policy from them (it never reads the database); the backend validates the saved settings against them. Set the same values on both. |\n| `FRONTEND_TRUSTED_IP_HEADER` | website | `x-nf-client-connection-ip` on Netlify, `x-real-ip` behind your own proxy. |\n\n### Tests and demo data\n\n`CI` with `ALLOW_INSECURE_PRODUCTION_TESTS=1` relaxes the TLS checks in CI only. `TEST_DATABASE_URL` points the\nintegration tests at a disposable database; `TEST_QUEUE_REDIS_URL` at a disposable Redis turns on the BullMQ suites. `DEMO_PASSWORD` and `DEMO_MFA_SECRET` feed `npm run db:seed-demo`.\n\n## Deployment (bundled `.env` and `cloud.env`)\n\n| Variable | Default | Description |\n|---|---|---|\n| `SWO_REPOSITORY` | `SpaceWorks-Labs/SpaceWorks-Organization` | Repository the installer, updater and runner follow (a fork works). |\n| `SWO_BACKEND_IMAGE`, `SWO_FRONTEND_IMAGE` | `ghcr.io/<owner>/spaceworks-organization-backend` / `-frontend` | Image names, derived from the repository owner. |\n| `SWO_IMAGE_TAG` | `.spaceworks-version`, else `latest` | The release the stack runs. |\n| `SWO_LAYER` | `none` | `none` plain HTTP through Caddy, `tls` automatic HTTPS, `proxy` your own proxy on `127.0.0.1:8080`. |\n| `SWO_STORAGE` | `bundled` | `external` drops Silo for the `S3_*` bucket. |\n| `PUBLIC_APP_BASE_URL` | | The public origin; becomes `AUTH_URL` and `NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_URL`. |\n| `SITE_ADDRESS` | | Caddy's site: `<domain>` for automatic HTTPS, `http://<host>` for plain HTTP. |\n| `TLS_EMAIL` | | Let's Encrypt account email (`SWO_LAYER=tls`). |\n| `HTTP_PORT`, `HTTP_PORT_BIND`, `TLS_HTTPS_PORT` | `80`, `0.0.0.0`, `443` | Caddy's published ports. |\n| `API_BIND`, `API_PORT` | `127.0.0.1`, `8080` | Where the app listens for your proxy (`SWO_LAYER=proxy`). |\n| `APP_BIND`, `APP_PORT` | `127.0.0.1`, `8080` | Where the cloud stack's app listens. |\n| `POSTGRES_PASSWORD` | generated | Owner role `sworg` (migrations, backups). |\n| `POSTGRES_APP_PASSWORD` | generated | Runtime role `sworg_app` (data only). |\n| `REDIS_PASSWORD` | generated | Redis `requirepass`. |\n| `MINIO_ROOT_USER`, `MINIO_ROOT_PASSWORD` | generated | Silo administrator. |\n| `S3_APP_PASSWORD` | generated | Secret of the `sworg-app` storage identity, limited to the bucket. |\n| `BACKUP_INTERVAL_HOURS` | `24` | Scheduled database dump and bucket mirror interval. |\n| `SILO_IMAGE_TAG` | pinned | Silo release. |\n\n## Backup settings (`backup.env`)\n\nWritten by the installer menu's Backups option (`scripts/vps-backup-settings.sh`) into the owner-only\n`backup.env` beside `.env`, and read only by `scripts/vps-offsite-backup.sh`. They are host settings, not\napplication environment, so they are in no `.env` or Compose file. See\n[DEPLOYMENT_VPS.md](DEPLOYMENT_VPS.md#encrypted-off-site-copies).\n\n| Variable | Description |\n|---|---|\n| `BACKUP_AGE_RECIPIENT` | age public key; off-site copies are encrypted to it. The private key stays off the server. |\n| `OFFSITE_ENDPOINT`, `OFFSITE_BUCKET`, `OFFSITE_PREFIX` | The off-site S3-compatible bucket and folder. |\n| `OFFSITE_ACCESS_KEY_ID`, `OFFSITE_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY` | A key limited to that bucket. |\n| `OFFSITE_KEEP_DAYS` | Days copies stay in the bucket (90). |\n| `OFFSITE_CA` | `offsite-ca.crt` when a private endpoint uses its own CA. |\n\n## Scripts and images\n\n| Variable | Used by | Description |\n|---|---|---|\n| `SWO_DIR` | `install.sh`, deploy job | Install directory (`/opt/spaceworks-organization`). |\n| `SWO_INSTALL_REPOSITORY_PINNED` | installer | Set when the repository was chosen before download. |\n| `SWO_STATIC_ENV_FILE` | wrapper | Settings file (`<install>/.env` bundled, `/etc/spaceworks-organization/cloud.env` cloud). |\n| `SWO_UPDATE_INSTALL_DIR` | wrapper, updater | The install a staged updater works on. |\n| `SWO_COMPOSE_BUILD_LAYER` | wrapper, `setup.sh --build` | `1` builds the backend image from the checkout. |\n| `SWO_BUILD_KEY` | wrapper | Throwaway Server Actions key for local builds (generated per run). |\n| `SWO_COMPOSE_PROJECT` | wrapper | Compose project (`sworg`, `sworg-cloud`, `sworg-smoke`). |\n| `SWO_TLS_DIR`, `SWO_BACKUP_DIR` | compose | Certificate and backup directories (`./tls`, `./backups`). |\n| `SWO_RUNNER_DIR` | `install-runner.sh`, `vps-status.sh`, `vps-uninstall.sh` | Runner directory (`/opt/spaceworks-organization-runner`). |\n| `SWO_DEPLOY` | GitHub repository variable | `true` enables the release workflow's `deploy` job. |\n| `SWO_TOPOLOGY` | ops role | `bundled` or `cloud`; the runtime-role check runs only for bundled. |\n| `SWO_NEXT_DIR` | entrypoint | Where the web build is (`/app/.next`, frontend `/app/frontend/.next`). |\n| `SWO_DISABLE_BUILD_CACHE`, `SWO_FRONTEND_STANDALONE` | Dockerfile | Image build switches. |\n| `SWO_SMOKE_PORT` | `compose-smoke.sh` | The smoke test's edge port (18080). |" },
  { slug: "deployment-vps", section: "operators", title: "Deploying on a server (VPS)", summary: "The bundled deployment runs the whole platform on one Linux server with Docker Compose, exactly like a standalone SpaceWorks install: the curl installer, setup.sh,…", body: "# Deploying on a server (VPS)\n\nThe bundled deployment runs the whole platform on one Linux server with Docker Compose, exactly like a\nstandalone SpaceWorks install: the curl installer, `setup.sh`, `scripts/update.sh` and\n`scripts/spaceworks-compose.sh bundled`. Assume a dedicated server: the installer's Caddy takes ports 80 and 443.\n\n## Install\n\n```bash\ncurl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/SpaceWorks-Labs/SpaceWorks-Organization/main/install.sh | bash\n```\n\nThe installer checks the host (architecture, distribution, Docker, disk space, an existing install), asks which\nGitHub repository to follow, downloads the newest published `main` release into temporary storage and asks the\ndeployment questions (see the README). Nothing changes until you confirm the summary. Then it installs missing\npackages (Docker Engine, the Compose plugin, curl, tar), copies the release to `/opt/spaceworks-organization`\nand runs `setup.sh`, which:\n\n1. writes the owner-only `.env` with every generated secret (`scripts/setup-write-environment.sh`);\n2. pulls the release images (or builds them with `bash setup.sh --build`);\n3. creates a private CA and service certificates for PostgreSQL, Redis and Silo in `tls/`\n   (`deploy/certificates.sh`, run inside the backend image so the host needs no openssl);\n4. starts the stack with `up --wait` and runs the operator checks (`--role ops`);\n5. installs the optional GitHub Actions runner.\n\nOn Linux with systemd the installer then schedules the weekly internal-certificate check (see\n[Certificates](#certificates)). Options: `--repo owner/name` (or `SWO_REPOSITORY`), `SWO_DIR=/path` for another\ninstall directory, `--help`.\n\n## The installer menu\n\nRun the same curl command on an installed server and it offers every operation. Each option runs a script of\nthe installed release as the install owner (with `sudo` only for host changes), so you can also run it\ndirectly from the install directory:\n\n| Option | Script | What it does |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 Update | `scripts/update.sh --force` | See [Updates and rollback](#updates-and-rollback). |\n| 2 Back up now | `scripts/backup.sh` | Dump, bucket mirror and settings archive into `backups/`. |\n| 3 Roll back | `scripts/update.sh --force --rollback` | The release before the last update. |\n| 4 Status and health | `scripts/vps-status.sh` | Release, disk, newest dumps, off-site status, timers, certificate expiry, every service's state and health (backend, worker, beat, db, redis, minio, caddy, backup), the public address, then the operator checks. Changes nothing. |\n| 5 Backups | `scripts/vps-backup-settings.sh` | Backup interval and retention, encryption key, off-site copies. |\n| 6 Change settings | `scripts/vps-reconfigure.sh` | Web address, outgoing email or external bucket keys, rolled back automatically if the checks fail. |\n| 7 Server security | `scripts/vps-harden.sh [--all]` | Firewall (SSH, HTTP, HTTPS only), automatic security updates, fail2ban. |\n| 8 Automatic deploys | `scripts/install-runner.sh [--remove]` | See [Updates and rollback](#updates-and-rollback). |\n| 9 Recover super-admin access | `scripts/vps-recover.sh` | Lost authenticator or password, through `scripts/admin.sh`. |\n| 10 Support bundle | `scripts/vps-support.sh` | Report and logs with secrets redacted, in `support/`. |\n| 11 Uninstall | `scripts/vps-uninstall.sh` | See [Uninstalling](#uninstalling). |\n\nA release older than an option says so; update first (option 1).\n\n## What runs\n\n`scripts/spaceworks-compose.sh bundled` always uses `docker-compose.prod.yml`, the install's `.env`, the\nproject name `sworg`, and the overlays the settings select:\n\n| Setting in `.env` | Overlay | Effect |\n|---|---|---|\n| `SWO_LAYER='none'` | none | Caddy serves `http://<host>` on port 80 (plain HTTP, LAN or trial). |\n| `SWO_LAYER='tls'` | `docker/compose.tls.yml` | Caddy obtains Let's Encrypt certificates and also serves 443 (TCP and QUIC). |\n| `SWO_LAYER='proxy'` | `docker/compose.proxy.yml` | No Caddy; the app listens on `127.0.0.1:8080` for your proxy. |\n| `SWO_STORAGE='external'` | `docker/compose.external-storage.yml` | No Silo; files go to the `S3_*` bucket. |\n| `SWO_COMPOSE_BUILD_LAYER=1` (shell) | `docker/compose.build.yml` | Builds the backend image from the checkout. |\n\n| Service | Image | Role |\n|---|---|---|\n| `db` | `postgres:16-alpine` | TLS only (`hostnossl … reject`), owner role `sworg`, runtime role `sworg_app` with data rights only |\n| `redis` | `redis:7-alpine` | TLS only, password, append-only file |\n| `minio` | `pgsty/silo` | TLS only, private bucket `spaceworks-organization`, versioning on |\n| `createbuckets` | `pgsty/silo` | Creates the bucket and the `sworg-app` identity limited to it, then exits |\n| `migrate` | backend, `--role migrate` | `prisma migrate deploy` as the owner role, then exits |\n| `backend` | backend, `--role backend` | The app and API (Next.js) as `sworg_app` |\n| `worker` | backend, `--role worker` | BullMQ worker (`dist/worker.js`): email and chat delivery, the membership sweep, payment checks, retention and every job in `lib/queue/registry.ts`; health on `:3460/health` |\n| `beat` | backend, `--role beat` | BullMQ beat (`dist/beat.js`): keeps the repeatable schedules in Redis (outbox and membership sweep every minute); health on `:3460/health` |\n| `backup`, `backup-storage` | `postgres:16-alpine`, `pgsty/silo` | Scheduled database dumps and bucket mirror into `backups/` |\n| `caddy` | `caddy:2-alpine` | The web edge; blocks `/api/internal/*`; overwrites `X-Real-IP` |\n| `ops` (profile `tools`) | backend, `--role ops` | Operator checks, run on demand |\n\nThe data services sit on an internal network with no route to the internet. The backend, worker and beat also\njoin the edge network, so they can reach SMTP relays, payment gateways and chat services. Only Caddy publishes\nports.\n\n```bash\nscripts/spaceworks-compose.sh bundled ps\nscripts/spaceworks-compose.sh bundled logs -f backend worker beat\nscripts/spaceworks-compose.sh bundled run --rm ops      # TLS, roles, migrations, Redis, bucket, readiness\n```\n\n## Files on the server\n\n| Path | Contents |\n|---|---|\n| `/opt/spaceworks-organization/` | The installed release (compose files, `deploy/`, `scripts/`) |\n| `.env` | Every setting and secret (mode 600). Back it up encrypted, off the server. |\n| `tls/` | The private CA (`ca.key` never leaves this folder) and the service certificates |\n| `backups/` | Database dumps, the bucket mirror, settings archives, replaced host files, `offsite-status` |\n| `backup.env` | Backup encryption and off-site settings (mode 600; written by menu option 5) |\n| `support/` | Support bundles (menu option 10) |\n| `.spaceworks-version`, `.spaceworks-previous-version` | The running release and the one before it |\n\n## Backups and restore\n\n- **Scheduled:** the `backup` service dumps the database every `BACKUP_INTERVAL_HOURS` (24) to\n  `backups/scheduled-<time>.sql.gz` and keeps `BACKUP_RETENTION_DAYS` (14); `backup-storage` mirrors the bucket\n  to `backups/storage`.\n- **On demand:** `bash scripts/backup.sh` (also the installer's menu option 2) writes a dump, refreshes the\n  bucket mirror and archives `.env` and `tls/` to `backups/config-<time>.tar.gz`.\n- **Before every update:** `backups/pre-update-<time>.sql.gz`.\n\nBackups contain every secret. Copy them off the server, encrypted: menu option 5 does it every night.\n\nThe backup service's interval and retention are `BACKUP_INTERVAL_HOURS` and `BACKUP_RETENTION_DAYS` in `.env`;\noption 5 changes them and restarts the services that read them.\n\n### Encrypted off-site copies\n\nOption 5 first makes an [age](https://age-encryption.org) key: the public half is saved in `backup.env`, the\nprivate half is shown once and never stored on the server. Keep it in a password manager. Then it asks for an\nS3-compatible bucket at another provider (endpoint, optional private CA, bucket, folder, a key limited to that\nbucket, days to keep), checks the bucket is reachable and schedules `sworg-offsite-backup.timer` (every night at\nabout 03:30 UTC). Each run of `scripts/vps-offsite-backup.sh` uploads a folder named after its UTC time:\n\n| File | Contents |\n|---|---|\n| `database.sql.gz.age` | A fresh dump, taken while the stack runs |\n| `settings.tar.gz.age` | `.env`, `tls/` and `backup.env` |\n| `storage.tar.age` | The bucket mirror (bundled Silo only; back up an external bucket with its provider) |\n\nEvery file is encrypted on the way out; nothing unencrypted leaves the server. Local `backups/` stay as they are\n(owner-only). Copies older than the chosen days are removed from the bucket. Option 4 shows the last result.\n\n```bash\nbash scripts/vps-offsite-backup.sh --fetch 20261029T033012Z      # into backups/offsite/20261029T033012Z/\ncd backups/offsite/20261029T033012Z\nage -d -i key.txt database.sql.gz.age > ../../offsite-20261029T033012Z.sql.gz\nage -d -i key.txt settings.tar.gz.age | tar -xz -C /somewhere/private\nage -d -i key.txt storage.tar.age | tar -x -C /somewhere/private   # files for backups/storage\n```\n\nThen restore the dump with `bash scripts/restore.sh backups/offsite-20261029T033012Z.sql.gz`. Remove `key.txt`\nfrom the server afterwards.\n\n```bash\nbash scripts/restore.sh backups/scheduled-20261020T020000Z.sql.gz                 # database only\nbash scripts/restore.sh backups/manual-20261020T090000Z.sql.gz --with-storage     # and the files\n```\n\nRestore asks you to type `RESTORE`, takes a fresh `pre-restore` dump, stops the app, worker, beat and edge,\nreplaces the schema in one transaction, optionally copies the files back, and starts everything again;\nmigrations bring an older dump up to the running release.\n\n### Restore on a new server\n\nInstall with the same answers, stop the stack, put the old `.env` and `tls/` back from the settings archive\n(or an off-site `settings.tar.gz.age`), start it, then restore the dump (and `backups/storage` with\n`--with-storage`).\n\n### Uninstalling\n\nOption 11 asks you to type `UNINSTALL`, then whether to erase everything (`DELETE DATA`). By default it takes a\nfinal backup, removes the runner registration, the timers, the containers, the images and the install\ndirectory, and keeps the data volumes (`sworg_*`) and `.env`, `tls/`, `backup.env`, `.spaceworks-version` and\n`backups/` in `/opt/spaceworks-organization.kept-<time>`. Docker and any server hardening stay.\n\n**Reinstall after uninstalling** with the kept data: download the kept release into the install directory, put\nthe kept files back and start it.\n\n```bash\nkept=$(ls -d /opt/spaceworks-organization.kept-* | tail -n 1)\nversion=$(cat \"$kept/.spaceworks-version\")\nsudo mkdir /opt/spaceworks-organization && sudo chown \"$(id -u):$(id -g)\" /opt/spaceworks-organization\ncurl -fsSL \"https://github.com/SpaceWorks-Labs/SpaceWorks-Organization/archive/refs/tags/v$version.tar.gz\" \\\n  | tar -xz --strip-components=1 -C /opt/spaceworks-organization\nsudo mv \"$kept\"/.env \"$kept\"/tls \"$kept\"/backups \"$kept\"/.spaceworks-version /opt/spaceworks-organization/\ncd /opt/spaceworks-organization && sudo chown -R \"$(id -u):$(id -g)\" .env tls .spaceworks-version\nscripts/spaceworks-compose.sh bundled up -d --wait && scripts/spaceworks-compose.sh bundled run --rm ops\n```\n\nFor a clean start instead, remove the volumes\n(`docker volume rm $(docker volume ls -q --filter label=com.docker.compose.project=sworg)`) and follow\n[Restore on a new server](#restore-on-a-new-server).\n\n## Super admins\n\nSuper admins sign in with their email and password only. Their accounts and passwords are managed on the\nserver, never by email and never in the app:\n\n```bash\nbash scripts/admin.sh create --email you@example.org          # make (or promote) a super admin; asks for a password\nbash scripts/admin.sh set-password --email you@example.org    # change a super admin's password; signs them out everywhere\nbash scripts/admin.sh reset-mfa --email you@example.org       # remove an authenticator from a super-admin account\nbash scripts/admin.sh revoke --email you@example.org          # take super-admin access away\nbash scripts/admin.sh list\n```\n\n**Lost access** (menu option 9, `scripts/vps-recover.sh`) lists the super admins, then removes a lost\nauthenticator, sets a new password with hidden input on this terminal, does both, or creates a super admin,\nall through `scripts/admin.sh`. It never sends or prints a reset link; each change signs the account out and is\naudited.\n\nThe installer creates the first one from the email and password you give. On an upgrade from 0.6 or earlier,\nthe existing `SUPER_ADMIN_EMAIL` account is kept as super admin automatically. Without Docker\n(`npm run admin -- …`) the same commands run against `DATABASE_URL`.\n\n## Updates and rollback\n\n```bash\nbash scripts/update.sh --force                                   # newest main release\nbash scripts/update.sh --force --version 0.8.0-main.42.a1b2c3d4e5f6\nbash scripts/update.sh --rollback                                # the release before the last update\n```\n\nThe updater takes a lock (`flock`, or a PID lock on Git Bash; `--override-lock` clears a verified stale one),\nresolves a published, non-prerelease `main` release, downloads its source and hands over to that release's own\nupdater. It dumps the database, checks the new image can be pulled, installs the release's host files (keeping\nthe old ones in `backups/host-config-*`), pulls the images, runs migrations and waits for every service to be\nhealthy, then runs the operator checks.\n\n- **Automatic rollback:** if the new release does not become healthy, the previous host files and images come\n  back with `--no-deps`, so older migrations never run against the newer schema.\n- **Manual rollback:** `--rollback` returns to the release recorded in `.spaceworks-previous-version`, again\n  without running migrations. Database changes stay; to return the data too, restore the pre-update dump the\n  command prints.\n\nThe installer menu offers both (options 1 and 3), and the release workflow's `deploy` job runs\n`update.sh --force --version <release>` on a server with the runner (`scripts/install-runner.sh`).\n\n## Certificates\n\nThe service certificates of PostgreSQL, Redis and Silo last 825 days (the private CA ten years). On Linux with\nsystemd the installer schedules `sworg-certificates.timer` (Mondays at about 04:00 UTC), which runs\n`scripts/vps-renew-certs.sh --if-due`: within 60 days of expiry it reissues them from the same CA, recreates the\ndata services, the app, worker, beat and Caddy, and runs the operator checks. Option 4 shows the expiry date.\n\n```bash\nbash scripts/vps-renew-certs.sh                   # renew now\nbash scripts/vps-renew-certs.sh --install-timer   # schedule the weekly check (installs made without the installer)\n```\n\nCaddy renews the public certificates by itself.\n\n## Changing settings\n\nOption 6 (`scripts/vps-reconfigure.sh`) changes the web address (host name or domain for the HTTPS mode chosen\nat install, and the Netlify website origin for a backend-only server), outgoing email (sender, SMTP host, port,\nlogin, password) or an external bucket's keys. It takes a `pre-settings` database dump, keeps `.env.previous`,\nwrites the new values, recreates the services and runs the operator checks; if anything fails, the previous\n`.env` comes back and the stack restarts. Changing the address signs everyone out, and links already sent keep\nthe old one. Changing the HTTPS mode or the storage is in [Moving between modes](#moving-between-modes).\n\n## Server security\n\nOption 7 (`scripts/vps-harden.sh`, `--all` without questions) asks for each step: ufw (Debian/Ubuntu) or\nfirewalld allowing only the SSH ports `sshd` listens on, HTTP and HTTPS (left alone if the SSH port cannot be\nfound); unattended-upgrades or dnf-automatic for security fixes; and fail2ban banning an address for an hour\nafter five failed SSH logins. Docker publishes its own ports past ufw; this stack publishes only 80 and 443, or\n`127.0.0.1:8080` behind your proxy.\n\n## Moving between modes\n\n- **Plain HTTP to automatic HTTPS:** point DNS at the server, then in `.env` set `SWO_LAYER='tls'`,\n  `SITE_ADDRESS='<domain>'`, `TLS_EMAIL='<you>'`, `PUBLIC_APP_BASE_URL='https://<domain>'` and remove\n  `ALLOW_PLAIN_HTTP`, then `scripts/spaceworks-compose.sh bundled up -d`.\n- **Website on Netlify:** set `DEPLOYMENT_MODE='backend'`, a 64-hex `FRONTEND_GATEWAY_SECRET` and\n  `PUBLIC_APP_BASE_URL` to the website's origin; see [DEPLOYMENT_NETLIFY.md](DEPLOYMENT_NETLIFY.md).\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n- Start with option 4 (`bash scripts/vps-status.sh`): it names every failing service and check. Option 10\n  (`bash scripts/vps-support.sh`) collects the same with the last 500 log lines of each service, secrets\n  redacted, into `support/`; read it before sharing, as logs can hold email and IP addresses.\n\n- `scripts/spaceworks-compose.sh bundled logs migrate` shows a failed migration; the app never starts on a\n  half-migrated database.\n- The worker and beat are healthy only while their Redis heartbeat is fresh; `logs worker beat` shows a\n  Redis or database connection problem. A worker that is up but sends nothing usually means beat is down:\n  check `ps` for both.\n- `run --rm ops` names the failing check without printing any connection detail; it also checks the worker's\n  and beat's `/health` on `JOBS_HEALTH_PORT`.\n\n## Sign-in options\n\nThe installer asks \"Which sign-in options besides email and password?\" (`google, apple, github, oidc, saml, supabase,\nfirebase`, or `none`, the default; every option is off until switched on) and then the ids each needs: Google and GitHub client id and secret, Apple Services ID, team ID, key ID and\nthe path to the `.p8` key, the Supabase project URL and publishable key, and the Firebase web app values and\nproviders. Everything goes into `.env` (mode 600); secrets are never printed. Picking options also writes\n`LOGIN_METHODS`, which then decides the switches. Change them later with\n`bash scripts/admin.sh login-methods --show` or `--set google,apple` (OpenID Connect and SAML providers themselves\nare added in Super admin, Auth) (remove `LOGIN_METHODS` from `.env` first, or\nthe server's list wins). Supabase needs asymmetric JWT signing keys and `/login/bridge/supabase` in its redirect\nallow-list. Details: `docs/CONFIGURATION.md`, `docs/SECURITY.md`." },
  { slug: "deployment-cloud", section: "operators", title: "Deploying with managed services (cloud)", summary: "The cloud topology runs only the application processes, all from the backend image, and leaves the data to managed services: PostgreSQL with TLS, Redis over TLS, an S3-compatible…", body: "# Deploying with managed services (cloud)\n\nThe cloud topology runs only the application processes, all from the backend image, and leaves the data to\nmanaged services: PostgreSQL with TLS, Redis over TLS, an S3-compatible bucket and an SMTP relay. It mirrors\nSpaceWorks' `docker-compose.cloud.yml` and `scripts/spaceworks-compose.sh cloud`.\n\n## What you need\n\n| Service | Examples | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| PostgreSQL 16 | Supabase, Neon, RDS, Cloud SQL | `sslmode=require` (or `verify-full`). Behind a transaction pooler (Supabase `:6543`) add `pgbouncer=true` to `DATABASE_URL` and give the direct or session URL as `DIRECT_URL` for migrations. |\n| Redis 7 | Upstash, ElastiCache, Redis Cloud | Must be `rediss://` (TLS). Holds rate limits and the BullMQ queues, so choose a plan without eviction (`noeviction`) and with room for steady polling by the worker. |\n| Object storage | Cloudflare R2, Amazon S3, Backblaze B2 | A **private** bucket and a key limited to it. `S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=1` works with all of them. |\n| SMTP | Brevo, Amazon SES, Postmark | A verified sending domain. |\n| HTTPS | Your platform's load balancer, Cloudflare, or a reverse proxy | Terminates TLS in front of `APP_PORT` and overwrites the client-IP header named in `TRUSTED_PROXY_IP_HEADER`. |\n\n### Harden the database connection\n\n`sslmode=require` encrypts the connection but doesn't check who is at the other end. In production:\n\n- **Verify the server.** Use `sslmode=verify-full` with your provider's CA (`sslrootcert=/path/to/ca.pem`, mounted\n  read-only into the app, worker and beat containers). Supabase, Neon and RDS all publish their CA bundles.\n- **Run the app as a data-only role.** Migrations need the schema owner; the running app doesn't. Create a second\n  role with `CONNECT`, `USAGE` on the schema and `SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE` on its tables (plus\n  `USAGE, SELECT` on sequences and `ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES` so new tables follow), put that role in\n  `DATABASE_URL`, and keep the owner only in `DIRECT_URL`, which only the `migrate` role uses. This is how the\n  bundled VPS stack runs (`sworg_app`), and it means a compromised app process can't alter the schema or the\n  payment triggers.\n\n### Object storage on Cloudflare R2\n\nCreate a bucket (keep public access off) and an R2 API token with **Object Read & Write** for that bucket only, then:\n\n```bash\nS3_ENDPOINT='https://<account-id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com'\nS3_REGION='auto'\nS3_BUCKET='spaceworks-organization'\nS3_ACCESS_KEY_ID='<token access key id>'\nS3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY='<token secret access key>'\nS3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE='1'\n```\n\nFiles are served through the application (`/api/uploads`), so the bucket needs no public URL or CORS rules. Member\nphotos sit under `organisations/<org>/members/<member>/`; cards are drawn on demand and never stored. Any other\nS3-compatible store works the same way (see [Configuration](CONFIGURATION.md#object-storage)).\n\n## Docker host with Compose\n\n```bash\ngit clone https://github.com/SpaceWorks-Labs/SpaceWorks-Organization.git /opt/spaceworks-organization\ncd /opt/spaceworks-organization\nsudo install -D -m 600 .env.production.example /etc/spaceworks-organization/cloud.env\nsudoedit /etc/spaceworks-organization/cloud.env      # fill every value; see docs/CONFIGURATION.md\nexport SWO_IMAGE_TAG=0.8.0-main.42.a1b2c3d4e5f6     # a published release, or latest\nscripts/spaceworks-compose.sh cloud pull\nscripts/spaceworks-compose.sh cloud up -d --wait\nscripts/spaceworks-compose.sh cloud run --rm ops     # TLS, migrations, Redis, bucket, readiness\n```\n\nGenerate the keys with `openssl rand -base64 32` (`NEXT_SERVER_ACTIONS_ENCRYPTION_KEY`, `MFA_ENCRYPTION_KEY`,\n`PAYMENT_SECRETS_KEY`) and `openssl rand -base64 48` (`AUTH_SECRET`, `CRON_SECRET`, `HEALTHCHECK_SECRET`).\nKeep an offline copy: without `MFA_ENCRYPTION_KEY` and `PAYMENT_SECRETS_KEY`, saved authenticator seeds and\ngateway keys cannot be read. `SWO_STATIC_ENV_FILE` points the wrapper at another settings file.\n\n| Service | Role |\n|---|---|\n| `migrate` | `--role migrate`: `prisma migrate deploy` on `DIRECT_URL` (falls back to `DATABASE_URL`), then exits |\n| `backend` | `--role backend`: the app and API on `${APP_BIND:-127.0.0.1}:${APP_PORT:-8080}` |\n| `worker` | `--role worker node dist/worker.js`: runs every queued job (email and chat delivery, the membership sweep, payment checks, retention, the registry in `lib/queue/registry.ts`); health on `:3460/health` |\n| `beat` | `--role beat node dist/beat.js`: keeps the repeatable schedules in Redis; health on `:3460/health` |\n| `ops` (profile `tools`) | `--role ops`: operator checks on demand |\n\nBlock `/api/internal/*` at your edge; the endpoints also refuse any request without `CRON_SECRET`. One\nbeat is enough; more workers share the load, and a second beat does no harm.\n\n**Updating:** back up the database with your provider (point-in-time recovery or a snapshot), then set the new\n`SWO_IMAGE_TAG` and run `pull` and `up -d --wait` again. `scripts/update.sh` is for the bundled topology.\n\n## Render\n\n[`render.yaml`](../render.yaml) defines a Docker web service (`sworg-backend`, health check `/api/health`,\n`MIGRATE_ON_START=1`) and two background workers, `sworg-worker` (`--role worker node dist/worker.js`) and\n`sworg-beat` (`--role beat node dist/beat.js`), sharing the `sworg-prod` environment group. Render builds the\nDockerfile's last stage with your real `NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_URL` and `NEXT_SERVER_ACTIONS_ENCRYPTION_KEY` as build\narguments. Render's own Key Value is plain `redis://`, which production refuses, so use a TLS Redis such as\nUpstash.\n\nRender background workers need a paid plan. Without them, keep at least the worker (it does the delivery) and\nreplace beat with the cron fallback below.\n\n## Without a beat process (cron fallback)\n\nHosts that cannot keep a long-running beat call two endpoints once a minute from any cron service (a Render cron\njob, GitHub Actions, a systemd timer):\n\n```bash\ncurl -fsS -X POST -H \"Authorization: Bearer $CRON_SECRET\" https://<backend>/api/internal/jobs\ncurl -fsS -X POST -H \"Authorization: Bearer $CRON_SECRET\" https://<backend>/api/internal/outbox\n```\n\n`/api/internal/jobs` queues the registry's due jobs and `/api/internal/outbox` queues due outbox rows; a worker\nstill has to run to do the work. Repeated calls within the same minute are harmless. Both answer 404 without\nthe secret, so the calls must reach the backend directly, not through an edge that blocks `/api/internal/*`.\n\n## Other platforms\n\nAny container platform works with the published image:\n\n- web: `ghcr.io/spaceworks-labs/spaceworks-organization-backend:<version>` with the default command\n  (`--role backend node server.js`) and the variables in [CONFIGURATION.md](CONFIGURATION.md);\n- a release or pre-deploy job: the same image with `--role migrate`;\n- a background worker: the same image with `--role worker node dist/worker.js`;\n- a second background process: the same image with `--role beat node dist/beat.js`, or the cron fallback above.\n\nThe worker and beat need the same settings as the web service (database, Redis, SMTP, storage, keys); they\nlisten on `JOBS_HEALTH_PORT` (3460) for `GET /health`, which a platform health check can use.\n\n## Website on Netlify\n\nSet `DEPLOYMENT_MODE=backend` and `FRONTEND_GATEWAY_SECRET` here, and `PUBLIC_APP_BASE_URL` to the website's\norigin; see [DEPLOYMENT_NETLIFY.md](DEPLOYMENT_NETLIFY.md).\n\n## Sign-in options\n\nSet the sign-in variables in the settings file or the platform's environment (see `docs/CONFIGURATION.md`):\n`AUTH_GOOGLE_ID`/`AUTH_GOOGLE_SECRET`, `AUTH_GITHUB_ID`/`AUTH_GITHUB_SECRET`, `AUTH_APPLE_ID`, `AUTH_APPLE_TEAM_ID`,\n`AUTH_APPLE_KEY_ID`, `AUTH_APPLE_PRIVATE_KEY`, `AUTH_SUPABASE_URL`, `AUTH_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY`, `AUTH_SUPABASE_PROVIDERS`,\n`AUTH_FIREBASE_API_KEY`, `AUTH_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN`, `AUTH_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID`, `AUTH_FIREBASE_APP_ID`,\n`AUTH_FIREBASE_PROVIDERS` and, to fix the list of methods, `LOGIN_METHODS`. On Netlify set the bridge variables on\nthe website too: its bridge pages and content security policy read them there." },
  { slug: "deployment-netlify", section: "operators", title: "Hosting the site on Netlify", summary: "Space Works Organization can run as one server (a VPS with the installer), or split: the public pages and dashboards render on Netlify, and every database, payment and email…", body: "# Hosting the site on Netlify\n\nSpace Works Organization can run as one server (a VPS with the installer), or **split**: the public pages and\ndashboards render on Netlify, and every database, payment and email operation runs on your backend server.\nThis guide covers the split setup, which is how the public landing page is hosted.\n\n## How the split works\n\n- The `frontend/` workspace builds the same UI as the main app. Its `proxy.ts` forwards page data reads and\n  Server Actions to the backend through a fixed allowlist (`frontend/catalog.json`), signed with a shared\n  secret. No database URL or payment secret is ever set on Netlify.\n- The landing page (`/`) needs no backend data, so it renders even while the backend is down. Sign-in,\n  `/start`, dashboards and member pages need the backend.\n\n## 1. Run the backend\n\nInstall the backend on a VPS with the one-line installer (see the README) and answer **Website: 2) No, backend\nonly**. It asks for the Netlify website's HTTPS origin, requires HTTPS for the backend's own domain, and writes\nto the install's `.env`:\n\n- `DEPLOYMENT_MODE=backend`, so it serves only the API and Server Actions.\n- `FRONTEND_GATEWAY_SECRET`: 64 random hex characters, also set on Netlify (copy it from `.env`).\n- `PUBLIC_APP_BASE_URL`: the public site address (the Netlify domain or your own), which becomes\n  `NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_URL` and `AUTH_URL`.\n\nIn the cloud topology set the same three values in `cloud.env` ([DEPLOYMENT_CLOUD.md](DEPLOYMENT_CLOUD.md)).\n\n## 2. Create the Netlify site\n\n1. In Netlify, add a new site from the GitHub repository `SpaceWorks-Labs/SpaceWorks-Organization`.\n2. **Base directory:** leave empty (the repository root). **Package directory:** `frontend`.\n3. The build command and publish directory come from `frontend/netlify.toml`.\n4. Set these environment variables in the Netlify UI (Site configuration → Environment variables):\n\n| Variable | Value |\n|---|---|\n| `BACKEND_ORIGIN` | Your backend's HTTPS origin, for example `https://api.example.org` |\n| `NEXT_PUBLIC_BACKEND_ORIGIN` | The same value as `BACKEND_ORIGIN` |\n| `NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_URL` | The site's public address, for example `https://space-works-organization.netlify.app` |\n| `FRONTEND_GATEWAY_SECRET` | The same secret as on the backend |\n| `NEXT_SERVER_ACTIONS_ENCRYPTION_KEY` | A stable base64 key of 16, 24 or 32 bytes (`openssl rand -base64 32`) |\n| `NEXT_PUBLIC_HOSTED_BY` | Optional footer credit, for example `Shaan Shoukath, Systems Engineer` |\n| `WEB_TELEMETRY_ALLOWED_ORIGINS` | Optional. Bare https origins approved for outside web analytics; set the same value on the backend. Leave unset to keep the strict policy. |\n| `WEB_TELEMETRY_MIXPANEL_TOKEN`, `WEB_TELEMETRY_MIXPANEL_API_HOST`, `WEB_TELEMETRY_MIXPANEL_SDK_HOST` | Optional Mixpanel project and hosts (see `docs/CONFIGURATION.md`); also set on the backend. |\n\n`FRONTEND_TRUSTED_IP_HEADER` and the Node version are already set in `frontend/netlify.toml`.\n\n5. Deploy. Public `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` values are inlined at build time, so redeploy after changing them.\n\n## 3. Check it\n\n- `/` shows the landing page with \"Hosted by …\" in the footer when `NEXT_PUBLIC_HOSTED_BY` is set.\n- `/login` signs in through the backend.\n- `/start` submits an application, and the backend's outbox emails the applicant.\n\n## The website as a container instead of Netlify\n\nThe same build is published as `ghcr.io/spaceworks-labs/spaceworks-organization-frontend`. Run it with the\nvariables in the table above and `FRONTEND_TRUSTED_IP_HEADER=x-real-ip` behind a proxy that overwrites\n`X-Real-IP`; the public origins are applied when the container starts, so one image serves any domain.\n\n## Hosting on one server instead\n\nAnswer **Website: 1) Yes** in the installer (`DEPLOYMENT_MODE` stays unset) and point your domain at the VPS; the same app then serves the landing\npage, dashboards and API together. `NEXT_PUBLIC_HOSTED_BY` works the same way there.\n\n## Sign-in options\n\nSet the sign-in variables in the settings file or the platform's environment (see `docs/CONFIGURATION.md`):\n`AUTH_GOOGLE_ID`/`AUTH_GOOGLE_SECRET`, `AUTH_GITHUB_ID`/`AUTH_GITHUB_SECRET`, `AUTH_APPLE_ID`, `AUTH_APPLE_TEAM_ID`,\n`AUTH_APPLE_KEY_ID`, `AUTH_APPLE_PRIVATE_KEY`, `AUTH_SUPABASE_URL`, `AUTH_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY`, `AUTH_SUPABASE_PROVIDERS`,\n`AUTH_FIREBASE_API_KEY`, `AUTH_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN`, `AUTH_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID`, `AUTH_FIREBASE_APP_ID`,\n`AUTH_FIREBASE_PROVIDERS` and, to fix the list of methods, `LOGIN_METHODS`. On Netlify set the bridge variables and\n`LOGIN_METHODS` on the website too: its content security policy reads them there (the bridge pages get their\nconfiguration from the backend)." },
  { slug: "security", section: "operators", title: "Security", summary: "How Space Works Organization protects an installation, and what an operator must do. Report a vulnerability privately through GitHub's Security → Report a vulnerability on…", body: "# Security\n\nHow Space Works Organization protects an installation, and what an operator must do. Report a vulnerability\nprivately through GitHub's **Security → Report a vulnerability** on\n[SpaceWorks-Labs/SpaceWorks-Organization](https://github.com/SpaceWorks-Labs/SpaceWorks-Organization), not in a\npublic issue.\n\n## Data in transit\n\n- **Bundled stack:** PostgreSQL (`hostnossl … reject`), Redis (`port 0`, TLS only) and Silo accept TLS only,\n  with certificates from a private CA created on the server (`deploy/certificates.sh`). Clients verify the\n  hostname (`sslaccept=strict`, `rediss://`, `NODE_EXTRA_CA_CERTS`). The data network is internal: the data\n  services have no route to the internet and publish no ports.\n- **Public edge:** Caddy with automatic HTTPS (Let's Encrypt), HSTS from the app, and `/api/internal/*`\n  blocked. Plain HTTP exists only for a LAN or trial and needs `ALLOW_PLAIN_HTTP=1`; the app refuses an\n  `http://` origin otherwise.\n- **Cloud:** production refuses database URLs without `sslmode=require|verify-ca|verify-full`, Redis without\n  `rediss://`, and a non-HTTPS `S3_ENDPOINT`.\n\n## Least privilege\n\n- The app connects as `sworg_app`, which can read and write rows but owns no table and cannot create\n  databases, roles or schema objects. Migrations run separately as the owner role `sworg`. The operator check\n  (`--role ops`) verifies this on every install and update.\n- The app's storage identity `sworg-app` can list, read, write and delete only in the private bucket; the\n  bucket has no anonymous access.\n- The backend and frontend images run as an unprivileged user; operator scripts and the Prisma CLI are\n  root-owned and read-only inside the image.\n\n## Secrets\n\n- The installer generates every secret on the server and writes them to the install's `.env` (mode 600).\n  Nothing secret is printed, passed on a command line, or baked into an image.\n- Release images are built with a throwaway Server Actions key and placeholder origins; web roles refuse to\n  start without the installation's own key.\n- Authenticator seeds (`MFA_ENCRYPTION_KEY`), payment gateway secrets (`PAYMENT_SECRETS_KEY`) and provider\n  secrets saved in Super admin → Settings are encrypted at rest. Losing those keys makes the saved values\n  unreadable: keep an encrypted, off-server copy of `.env` and `tls/` (`bash scripts/backup.sh` archives them).\n- Backups contain personal data and every secret. Copy them off the server encrypted, and restrict who can\n  read `backups/`.\n- Internal job (`CRON_SECRET`: `/api/internal/jobs`, `/api/internal/outbox`) and readiness (`HEALTHCHECK_SECRET`) endpoints answer 404 without their bearer\n  secret.\n\n### Email providers and webhooks\n\n- Email provider secrets (SES secret access key, Resend API key, Postmark server token, webhook secrets) and chat\n  destinations, including a signed webhook's address and secret, are encrypted at rest and write-only: no reply,\n  log line, audit row or URL carries one, and a saved value is only ever shown as \"saved\". Settings changes are\n  audited by field name.\n- Bounce and complaint reports arrive at `/api/email/webhooks/{ses|resend|postmark}` and are verified before the\n  body is read: Resend's Svix HMAC-SHA256 signature (five-minute tolerance against replay), Postmark's basic auth\n  password compared in constant time with the saved secret, and SES through Amazon SNS signature verification. We\n  chose SNS verification over a secret URL token, and it needs two safeguards beyond the signature: the signing\n  certificate may only come from an `sns.<region>.amazonaws.com` HTTPS address (no redirects), and the message must\n  come from the one SNS topic pinned in settings, because any AWS account can produce a validly signed message\n  from its own topic. Without a pinned topic SES reports are refused. Unverified requests get 401 and change nothing.\n- Provider hosts are fixed (Resend, Postmark, the regional SES endpoint built from a validated region) and used only\n  over HTTPS without redirects. Hosts an organisation types in (SMTP, a signed webhook, Mattermost) must resolve to\n  public addresses, checked when saving and when connecting.\n\n## Releases and automatic deploys\n\n- The installer and updater accept only published, non-prerelease `main` releases whose tag matches\n  `v<VERSION>-main.<run>.<sha>`, and pin the images to that exact version.\n- This repository is public, and GitHub recommends self-hosted runners only for private repositories. The\n  deploy runner controls Docker, which is root on the server. Before setting `SWO_DEPLOY=true`:\n  - require approval for **all external contributors'** workflow runs;\n  - protect `main` so only maintainers can merge;\n  - in an organisation, limit the runner group to this repository (and to `release.yml` on\n    `refs/heads/main` where your plan allows);\n  - never add the `spaceworks-organization-prod` label to a `pull_request` workflow.\n  The deploy job uses no checkout and no third-party actions. Keep runner auto-updates on.\n- `scripts/install-runner.sh` verifies the runner download against GitHub's published SHA-256 and never echoes\n  or stores the registration token.\n\n## Application\n\n- Tenant isolation in every query; cross-tenant access returns 404.\n- Rate limits on sign-in and sensitive actions (Redis), with the client address taken only from the header\n  your edge overwrites (`TRUSTED_PROXY_IP_HEADER`).\n- Money rows are never edited or deleted; reversals use credit notes, and receipt numbers are gapless.\n\n### Card scans (`/verify`)\n\n- **Public check.** Anyone who scans a card's QR sees its verdict (current, renewal due, not started, not current,\n  revoked) with the member's name, plan, card number and valid-until date, never contact details. While the card is\n  live (not revoked or replaced), the membership is current or in grace, the member is not erased and the\n  organisation is active, the page also shows the card itself, front and back. It is drawn on demand by\n  `/api/verify/<token>/card` (`lib/cards/publicCard.ts`), never stored, sent `private, no-store`, limited per address\n  (`verifyCardImage`), and any other token gets the same 404.\n- **Staff scans.** Signed-in staff of the card's organisation who read members, with an authenticator and the\n  member inside their unit scope, also see that organisation's record of the member: memberships, payments and\n  cards, with contact details only for roles holding `members.contact`. The buttons call the dashboard's own server\n  actions, which check the organisation, unit and permission again. Each scan writes `CARD_SCANNED` (throttled);\n  a super admin's also writes `SUPER_ADMIN_ORG_ENTERED`. Staff of another organisation, or limited to another unit,\n  see exactly the public check, so a scan never shows that someone is a member elsewhere. Staff without an\n  authenticator are asked to set one up instead.\n\n### Sign-in\n\n- **Staff need an authenticator.** Anyone on an organisation's team (whatever their role) must\n  turn on an authenticator app before the dashboard, its actions or its exports open (`hasAuthenticator` in\n  `lib/permissions.ts`). Members never need one.\n- **Super admins** sign in with email and password only. Their accounts and passwords are created and changed\n  only on the server (`scripts/admin.sh`); password-reset emails, in-app password changes and Google, GitHub or\n  Apple sign-in are refused for them.\n- **Phone codes.** Off by default, and only with an SMS provider set up (`docs/NOTIFICATIONS.md`, \"Text messages\"). Only a number a signed-in person verified can sign in, and one number belongs to one account; the method never creates an account, refuses super admins, and an account with an authenticator also needs its code. Codes are six digits, valid ten minutes, single use, at most five tries, and kept only as an HMAC with `AUTH_SECRET` (as is the number, in the send log). Asking for a code is limited per number (3 in 15 minutes, 10 a day), per country calling code (`SMS_DAILY_COUNTRY_CAP`, default 500 a day, across the install) and per address; a number with no account is counted and answered exactly like one with an account, so the answer (200, or 429 with `Retry-After`) never shows whether an account exists. Wrong codes feed the same per-number delay and address block as passwords. Adding or removing a number needs a recent sign-in, writes `PHONE_ADDED` or `PHONE_REMOVED` without the number, and removal is refused when it is the last working way to sign in. It needs no email service.\n- **Platform access.** By the owner's decision (2026-10-29), super admins have full owner-level access to every\n  organisation's dashboard, with no session or time limit (`platformAccess` in `lib/permissions.ts`). Safeguards:\n  - they need an authenticator before an organisation's dashboard opens, like all staff;\n  - every action writes `SUPER_ADMIN_ORG_ACTION` and entering an organisation writes `SUPER_ADMIN_ORG_ENTERED`\n    (throttled), and the organisation's activity report lists them as \"Platform super admin\";\n  - a banner shows on every page they open there;\n  - they are never on the team: not listed, not counted against limits, not emailed. Money stays under the payment\n    triggers (paid rows are never edited or deleted).\n  A compromised super-admin account reaches every organisation, so keep super admins few and their authenticators\n  on.\n- **Sign-in methods.** One platform switch per method (password, Google, Apple, GitHub, OpenID Connect, SAML,\n  Supabase, Firebase, passkeys, phone codes) sits in front of that method's\n  configuration. **Password is on by default and every other method is off until the operator switches it on**\n  (product rules §15). A method works only when its switch is on and it is configured; switching one off never\n  deletes its credentials. OpenID Connect and SAML count as configured when at least one provider is enabled; each\n  provider's `enabled` flag is its own switch. `LOGIN_METHODS` in the environment overrides the switches\n  (`lib/loginMethods.ts`). With password sign-in off, the sign-in page hides the password form and sign-up, and the\n  server refuses a password sign-in from anyone but a super admin, who reaches the form at `/login?admin=1`.\n  Changes are audited as `LOGIN_METHODS_UPDATED` (field names only).\n- **Off means gone.** A method that is off is hidden on every screen and refuses with a typed error: the\n  credentials-type methods (password, SAML, Supabase, Firebase, passkeys) throw `method_disabled` from `authorize`; the\n  Google, Apple, GitHub and OpenID Connect start and callback URLs answer with `/login?error=method_disabled`\n  (`lib/authMethods/gate.ts`). The provider modules live in `lib/authMethods/`, one per method family.\n- **Passkeys** (WebAuthn; library choice in `docs/decisions.md`). Off by default; the switch is Auth in Super admin. A passkey is\n  registered only from a signed-in session with a recent sign-in (account Security page), with user verification\n  required and a discoverable credential preferred; the response is checked against our own challenge, the site's exact\n  origin and its hostname as the relying party id (taken from `NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_URL`, never the request). Only the public\n  key is stored. Challenges (`PasskeyChallenge`) are random, last five minutes and are used exactly once; a sign-in\n  challenge is named by an opaque httpOnly cookie. Sign-in asks for no email: the credential id finds the account. The\n  signature counter must go up when either side counts; a counter that stands still or goes back is refused and audited\n  as `PASSKEY_COUNTER_ROLLBACK` (a cloned authenticator). **A passkey never replaces the authenticator for staff**:\n  anyone on a team who has an authenticator still gives its code (or a recovery code) after the passkey; for members a\n  verified passkey counts as two steps. **Super admins have no passkeys** (password only, and a planted one is refused).\n  A passkey is a working way to sign in only while the method is on, and the last working way cannot be removed\n  (`countWorkingMethods`). Failures count towards the address block and the per-account delay. Adding and removing\n  are audited as `PASSKEY_ADDED` and `PASSKEY_REMOVED`. WebAuthn runs in the browser against the device, so it needs no\n  `connect-src` entry or other CSP change. Passkeys are tied to the domain: changing it makes them unusable, and\n  people fall back to their other methods.\n- **OpenID Connect.** Operators add any number of providers (Super admin, Auth): issuer (https), client id, a\n  write-only client secret (encrypted like other instance secrets, never selected into a page, a log or an audit\n  row), scopes. Auth.js does issuer discovery with PKCE, state and nonce, entirely through server-side redirects, so\n  no `connect-src` entry is needed. The `email_verified` claim must be true. **An OpenID Connect identity never links to\n  an existing account by email** (only Google, Apple and GitHub do): an email that already has an account and no link\n  for that provider and subject is refused with `link_from_account`; otherwise the same rules as every outside\n  identity apply (super admins refused, account creation gated by the sign-up switch or an invite). \"Test\n  connection\" fetches the discovery document over TLS and checks that its issuer matches. Changes are audited as\n  `OIDC_PROVIDER_CREATED`, `OIDC_PROVIDER_UPDATED` (field names) and `OIDC_PROVIDER_REMOVED`.\n- **SAML 2.0** (library choice in `docs/decisions.md`). Sign-in starts at `/api/auth/saml/<name>/login` (an\n  AuthnRequest by redirect; the RelayState is the same-site return path, signed and valid 10 minutes). The identity\n  provider posts its response to `/api/auth/saml/<name>/acs`, where the library checks the signature (the assertion\n  must be signed by default), issuer, audience, `NotBefore`/`NotOnOrAfter` and `InResponseTo` against a request id\n  stored in the database (single use, 10 minutes, bound to the provider; identity-provider-initiated responses are\n  refused), and we check the `Recipient`. The email is the NameID (email format) or a mail attribute. A valid response\n  becomes a one-time ticket (random 32 bytes, stored hashed, two minutes, single use) in the URL fragment of\n  `/login/saml`, which posts it to the Auth.js `saml` provider. The same linking rule as OpenID Connect applies: no\n  email linking, `link_from_account`. Metadata is served at `/api/auth/saml/<name>/metadata` (public setup\n  information, also while a provider is not yet enabled). The website's proxy lets only this one cross-site POST\n  through (`lib/frontend/httpProxy.ts`). Super admins are refused, as with every outside identity.\n- **Sign-in is bound to the browser that started it** (SAML and Supabase). The SAML login route sets `swo.saml-flow`\n  (random, httpOnly, Secure in production, SameSite Lax, 10 minutes, path `/api/auth`) and stores its SHA-256 on the\n  AuthnRequest row; the ACS copies that hash onto the ticket; `authorizeSaml` refuses (`saml_flow`, audited) when the\n  cookie is missing and `consumeSamlTicket` takes the ticket only when the cookie's hash matches (a wrong cookie does\n  not use the ticket up). A ticket or response planted in another browser therefore signs nobody in (login CSRF) and\n  cannot link an identity through a live link intent. The Supabase bridge uses PKCE (`flowType: \"pkce\"`): the verifier\n  lives in the starting browser's storage, a returning `?code=` is exchanged only where that verifier exists, and an\n  implicit `#access_token=` is never finished (`components/auth/supabaseReturn.ts`). Server-side token checks are unchanged.\n- **Identity-provider links belong to the provider row, not its name.** `Account.provider` is `oidc-<id>` or `saml-<id>`\n  (the row's immutable id; the Auth.js OIDC provider id and callback use it too). Deleting a provider deletes its account\n  links in the same transaction and audits the count (`linksRemoved`). Changing an OIDC issuer, or a SAML entity id or\n  sign-on address, is refused while any account is linked (\"Remove the linked accounts first, or add a new provider\"), so a\n  replaced identity provider's users can never reach the accounts of the old one. A newly added provider has a new id.\n- **Saved secrets are reused only for the target they were saved with.** The organisation SMTP password is reused only\n  when host, port and user all equal the saved values (otherwise it must be typed again, and a changed target without a\n  user clears it); ports are limited to 25, 465, 587 and 2525 for test and save, and a test is audited as\n  `ORG_EMAIL_TEST_SENT` (provider only). The platform SMTP password follows the same rule (service, host, port, user).\n  A saved SES secret is not reused with another region or access key id, nor a Twilio auth token with another account\n  SID or an API key secret with another key SID (`secretToRetype`, `smsSecretsToRetype`). Resend, Postmark, MSG91 and\n  Twilio have fixed hosts, so their other secrets carry over.\n- **Provider webhooks behind the split-hosting proxy.** `POST /api/email/webhooks/<provider>` and\n  `/api/sms/webhooks/<provider>` are exempt from the Origin check (like the SAML ACS), and for those paths only the\n  proxy forwards `svix-id`, `svix-timestamp`, `svix-signature`, `x-twilio-signature` and `x-amz-sns-*` (`authorization`\n  is already forwarded for Postmark). The backend verifies every one. The Twilio signature is computed over\n  `NEXT_PUBLIC_BASE_URL` + path + query, the address Twilio called.\n- **Pre-registered accounts.** Google, Apple and GitHub link by verified email, but not into an existing account that has\n  no password and whose identities are all single sign-on (SAML, OpenID Connect) or bridge accounts: `link_from_account`.\n  The inbox's owner signs in the original way and links the provider from the security page.\n- **Throttling.** Beyond the per-address block below, each email address has a growing delay (`lib/signInBackoff.ts`):\n  after 5 failed sign-ins or codes, further attempts are refused for 15 seconds, doubling to 15 minutes, cleared by a\n  correct sign-in. It is keyed by the lowercased email whether or not an account exists, so the answer never shows\n  whether one does (without Redis, the in-memory fallback sweeps expired entries and is capped at 10,000, oldest first). Auth.js refuses with `throttled`; the SAML routes answer 429 with `Retry-After`. Password reset\n  requests keep their own limit (5 an hour per address and per client).\n- **Without email.** When no email service is set up (`lib/emailAvailability.ts`: Super admin settings or the\n  server's SMTP or provider variables), the sign-in page hides \"Forgot password?\", requesting a reset is refused with\n  a message saying so, and **the emailed code for a new browser is skipped** so nobody is locked out. A password is\n  then reset by an administrator. Two-step sign-in with an authenticator is unaffected.\n- **Audit.** `SIGN_IN_SUCCEEDED` (method), `SIGN_IN_FAILED` (method and a reason code only, one row per method and\n  reason a minute, never an email or credential), `SIGN_OUT`, `MFA_ENABLED`, `MFA_DISABLED`, `RECOVERY_CODE_USED`,\n  `IDENTITY_LINKED`, `IDENTITY_UNLINKED`, `SESSION_REVOKED` (method only), `SESSIONS_REVOKED` (signed out everywhere),\n  `RECOVERY_CODES_REGENERATED` and every change to the method settings.\n- **Sessions and signed-in devices** (product rules §15). Each sign-in writes a `UserSession` row (`lib/userSessions.ts`)\n  and the JWT carries its id (`sid`). The `jwt` callback checks that row on every request, in the same indexed lookup\n  that reads the user's `sessionVersion`: a missing row, another user's, a revoked one or one made under an older version\n  signs the person out. `lastSeenAt` is written at most once every five minutes; rows unused for 60 days are dropped as\n  people sign in. A row stores a short device label (\"Firefox on Linux\", never the user agent string), the method, the\n  known device and an address prefix only (the first two octets of an IPv4 address, the first /48 of an IPv6 one),\n  never the address. Account, security → Signed-in devices lists them (this device marked) and signs one out\n  (`revokeSession`: only the caller's own row; anything else gets the same \"not found\"). Sign out everywhere revokes every\n  row and bumps `sessionVersion` in one transaction, so password resets, authenticator changes and the old mechanism\n  still work. Because the check runs inside Auth.js, it runs on the backend in split hosting, where `auth()` runs.\n- **Linking sign-in methods** (product rules §15). A signed-in person adds Google, GitHub, an OpenID Connect or SAML\n  provider, or a Supabase or Firebase account from Account, security → Sign-in methods, and removes any of them again.\n  Adding needs a recent sign-in and starts with a server action that sets `swo.link-intent`: an httpOnly cookie, ten\n  minutes, signed with a key derived from `AUTH_SECRET`, naming only the user and the provider (`lib/linkIntent.ts`).\n  - **OAuth and OpenID Connect.** Auth.js already attaches a new identity to the account its own session cookie decodes\n    to, but without asking whether that session still counts. The sign-in callback therefore runs\n    `oauthSessionGate` (`lib/authMethods/sessionGate.ts`) first: it reads the cookie and the session row itself. A live\n    session links only with a valid intent for that user and provider, and an identity linked to another account is\n    refused (`identity_linked_elsewhere`). A signed-in browser with no intent is turned back to the security page. A\n    revoked session never links anything: the dead cookie is cleared and the person asks again. Linking keeps the\n    browser's session and writes `IDENTITY_LINKED`, not a new sign-in or a new-device alert.\n  - **SAML and the bridges.** Their `authorize` finds the same intent and a live session of that user and links the\n    verified identity to that user (`linkExternalIdentity`), never by email.\n  - **Never for super admins** (password only), never moving an identity from one account to another, and Apple is not\n    offered: its cross-site form post drops the same-site session cookie linking relies on.\n  - **Unlinking** (`unlinkSignInMethod`) needs a recent sign-in, locks the user row, and is refused when it would leave no\n    working way to sign in: `countWorkingMethods` (`lib/authMethods/workingMethods.ts`) counts the password, linked\n    identities whose method is on, passkeys and the verified phone, each only while its method is switched on.\n- **Recovery codes.** The authenticator's one-time recovery codes are stored only as hashes and used once. They can be\n  replaced under Security (`regenerateRecoveryCodes`): it needs a recent sign-in and the authenticator's current code (never\n  a recovery code), replaces the whole set, shows the new codes once and writes `RECOVERY_CODES_REGENERATED`.\n- **Supabase and Firebase bridges.** The browser signs in with the provider and posts its token to an Auth.js\n  Credentials provider (`supabase` or `firebase`); the server verifies it with `jose` (`lib/bridgeTokens.ts`) before\n  anything else. Firebase: RS256 against Google's securetoken keys, issuer `https://securetoken.google.com/<project>`,\n  audience the project id, sane `exp`, `iat` and `auth_time`, an email, and `email_verified` true. Supabase: the\n  project's JWKS (asymmetric keys only; HS256 projects are refused), issuer `<url>/auth/v1`, audience\n  `authenticated`, then `<url>/auth/v1/user` must report the same user with a confirmed email. Phone-only identities\n  are refused. The verified, lowercased email then follows the same rules as Google, Apple and GitHub\n  (`lib/signInRules.ts`): super admins refused, an unverified password account protected, account creation gated by\n  the sign-up switch or an invite. Rate limits and automatic address blocking apply. The configuration is public\n  values from the environment, never secrets.\n  - **A bridge never links to an existing account by email.** It creates an account (with the provider's `sub`\n    stored as its Account, in one transaction) and later signs in only the account linked to that `sub`. An email\n    that already has an account here is refused (`bridge_link_refused`). Reason: a Supabase or Firebase project can\n    confirm addresses it never checked (for example Supabase with \"Confirm email\" off, where anyone holding the public\n    key can sign up as any address), which would otherwise hand over the existing account, authenticator and all.\n  - **Keep \"Confirm email\" on** in a Supabase project used here, and email verification on in Firebase, so new\n    accounts created through a bridge really belong to their address.\n  - A token naming an unknown signing key refetches the provider's key set at most once a minute.\n- **Apple's cross-site callback.** Apple answers with a form POST from another site, so while Apple sign-in is on\n  (HTTPS production) the Auth.js state, nonce, PKCE and callback cookies are `SameSite=None; Secure`. Apple sends the\n  name on the first sign-in only; its private-relay addresses count as verified.\n- **Unusual sign-ins.** Each browser that signs in is remembered by a long-lived random cookie, stored only as a\n  hash (`KnownDevice`). An account without an authenticator signing in from a new browser must enter a six-digit\n  code emailed to it (valid 10 minutes, single use), unless it verified its email in the last hour or no email\n  service is set up.\n- **New-device alerts.** Every first sign-in from a new device emails the account its browser, address and time.\n- **Automatic address blocking.** Twenty failed sign-ins (wrong password or code) from one address within\n  15 minutes block it from signing in, signing up and requesting password resets for an hour; again within a\n  week, for a day. Super admin, Security lists blocks and lifts them (`lib/ipBlock.ts`).\n- **Breached passwords.** New passwords (sign-up, change, reset, the server command) found in known breaches are\n  refused, via Have I Been Pwned with k-anonymity: only the first five characters of the SHA-1 hash leave the\n  server. `PWNED_PASSWORDS_CHECK=0` turns it off for offline installs.\n- **Staff invite links.** Invites (`/join/org/<token>`) are single use, can expire, and are revoked by the\n  organisation. A signed-out visitor can create an account on the invite page (password, or Google, Apple or\n  GitHub when enabled).\n  - **While \"new accounts\" is switched off,** opening a valid invite sets `swo.invite-signup`: an httpOnly cookie\n    lasting 30 minutes, signed with a key derived from `AUTH_SECRET`, naming only the invite token. It is\n    `SameSite=None; Secure` over HTTPS in production (Apple returns with a cross-site form POST) and `Lax` otherwise.\n  - **The cookie lifts only the sign-up switch,** and only after the database confirms the invite is still unused,\n    unexpired and, for an invite bound to an email, that the new account has that email.\n  - **One account per invite.** Creating the account claims the invite for that email in one atomic update\n    (`TeamInvite.signupEmail`), so however many people hold the link, it creates at most one account; the same\n    person retrying is answered as before. The cookie grants no role and never makes a super admin; accepting still goes through `acceptOrganisationInviteToken`, audited as before.\n  - **Verification returns only to an invite page.** The email verification link may carry a return path, accepted\n    only if it is a same-site `/join/org/<token>` path.\n- **Organisation-entered hosts** (an organisation's SMTP relay, a self-hosted Mattermost) must resolve to public\n  addresses, checked when saved and again when used, so they can't reach the server's own network.\n\n## Content Security Policy and outside web analytics\n\nThe policy is built per request in `lib/pageSecurity.ts` from the environment only (the proxies never read the database).\n\n- **`connect-src`** is `'self'`, the backend origin (`NEXT_PUBLIC_BACKEND_ORIGIN`, when the browser talks to it), the\n  Razorpay hosts on `/account` and `/pay/` only, and, on the approved public pages only, the operator's\n  `WEB_TELEMETRY_ALLOWED_ORIGINS` and the Mixpanel hosts while `WEB_TELEMETRY_MIXPANEL_TOKEN` is set. It never widens on\n  signed-in, token, pay, verify or sign-in pages, and not at all when nothing is configured. Stripe is a redirect\n  to Stripe's own checkout and needs no entry.\n- **Identity bridges** add hosts only on `/login`, `/login/bridge/*`, `/join/org/*` and `/verify-email`, only while\n  configured and not left out by `LOGIN_METHODS`: the Supabase project origin in `connect-src`; for Firebase,\n  `identitytoolkit.googleapis.com`, `securetoken.googleapis.com` and `www.googleapis.com` in `connect-src`, and the auth\n  domain and `apis.google.com` in `frame-src`. Card scans (`/verify/<token>`) and everything else stay strict.\n- **`frame-src`** allows Razorpay Checkout on `/account` and `/pay/`, the Firebase auth domain on the sign-in pages when Firebase is configured, and is `'none'` elsewhere.\n- **`'strict-dynamic'` makes `script-src` host lists inert** in current browsers: a script a nonced script creates is\n  trusted whatever its host. The real limits on outside providers are therefore the consent gate (nothing loads before\n  the visitor agrees, enforced by `lib/telemetry/controller.ts`) and `connect-src`. The host list is kept for browsers\n  that ignore `'strict-dynamic'`.\n- **Known gap:** `img-src` and `font-src` allow `https:` because organisation logos, member photos and fonts may be\n  served from any https host. A script that is already running could send data to an https host as an image or font\n  request. Closing it needs those assets proxied or allow-listed, which is not done.\n- **What an operator must list.** A vendor works only when its hosts are in `WEB_TELEMETRY_ALLOWED_ORIGINS`, for\n  example Google Analytics: `https://www.googletagmanager.com` and the regional `https://*.google-analytics.com`\n  hosts you see in the browser's network panel (wildcards are not accepted, list exact origins); Clarity:\n  `https://www.clarity.ms` and the collector hosts it reports; Sentry: the DSN's ingest origin; Firebase: the hosts\n  it reports; PostHog, Plausible and Umami: the collector origin. A blocked request is a missing origin, not a bug.\n- **Anonymous only.** The telemetry configuration is returned only to a request with no session, only the approved\n  public templates are reported (`lib/telemetry/routes.ts`), and a document that ever ran a provider is replaced\n  by a full page load before it can reach any other page. Sign-in and sign-out end with a full page load.\n- **Per-organisation providers.** An organisation can set its own providers (`Organisation.webTelemetry`, edited at\n  `/dashboard/<org>/settings/analytics` by the Owner, or by a super admin acting as the Owner, audited as\n  `ORG_WEB_TELEMETRY_UPDATED` with field names only). It uses the platform schema (public ids, origins from the\n  operator allow-list) and never Mixpanel. It runs only on that organisation's `/o/<slug>` pages and join form, for\n  signed-out visitors, with consent scoped `org:<id>`; the platform configuration does not run on those pages, so a\n  page never runs two page-analytics providers. An organisation that is not active has none.\n- **Visit counts (`POST /api/visits`).** Always on, and personal data is never stored: a request carries only an\n  approved route template (and an organisation slug under `/o/`), and the server adds one to a per-day counter\n  (`WebVisitDaily`). No cookie or storage key is written, and the address is never stored; it is hashed for the\n  per-address rate limit only (Redis, `apiRead`). Tokens and private routes have no template and are dropped, as are\n  unknown or inactive organisations and obvious bots by user agent. The answer is always 204, so a caller learns\n  nothing about why a visit was or was not counted." },
  { slug: "payments-operators", section: "operators", title: "Payments", summary: "How members pay, how money is recorded, and how receipts are issued. Tax rules are in GST.md.", body: "# Payments\n\nHow members pay, how money is recorded, and how receipts are issued. Tax rules are in [GST.md](GST.md).\n\n## Ways to pay\n\nEach organisation picks **one** gateway, or none:\n\n| Gateway | How the member pays | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Razorpay | Checkout.js opens over the pay page (UPI, cards, netbanking) | Orders API. INR, plus the international currencies the Razorpay account has enabled. |\n| Stripe | Redirect to Stripe Checkout and back | Checkout Sessions (`mode: payment`) on the organisation's **own** Stripe account. No Stripe script runs on our pages. Takes every supported currency; three-decimal currencies must be multiples of 10. New Stripe accounts in India may be invite-only. |\n| Offline | UPI QR, bank transfer, cash, cheque | Always available. A manager records it, or the member submits \"I've paid\" with a UTR. |\n\nKeys are saved under **Settings → Payments and tax**. Secrets are encrypted with AES-256-GCM using\n`PAYMENT_SECRETS_KEY` (32 random bytes, base64), bound to the organisation, and never sent back to the browser.\nWithout that key the gateway section stays locked. Test mode follows the key (`rzp_test_`, `sk_test_`).\n\n## The payment record\n\nEvery charge starts as a `Payment` with its `PaymentLine`s (term fee, joining or rejoining fee) and a frozen\n`taxSnapshot`, **before** any gateway order exists. So the amount, tax and receipt are identical whichever way\nthe member pays.\n\n| Status | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| `PENDING` | A gateway attempt was started |\n| `AWAITING_CONFIRMATION` | Offline money waiting for a manager (maker-checker, or a member's UTR claim) |\n| `PAID` | Money received; document issued (or held, see below) |\n| `WAIVED` | The fee was forgiven; no document, the amount forgiven is kept for reports |\n| `FAILED` | The gateway failed or the claim was rejected |\n| `CANCELLED` | Replaced by a newer attempt, or cancelled by a manager |\n\nOne open payment per term (a partial unique index); a new checkout cancels the previous attempt.\n\n## Settling\n\n`settlePayment` runs in one transaction: lock the payment, mark it `PAID`, activate the term\n(`markTermPaid`), allocate the document number and insert the document. Lock order is always\n**payment → term → document counter**.\n\nMoney received is **never refused**. These still become `PAID` with a document, and are flagged for review:\n\n- paid after the attempt was cancelled or failed (late capture);\n- the membership was already paid (duplicate);\n- the organisation was suspended or the plan archived.\n\nA **mismatched amount** is also `PAID` and flagged, but its document is **held**. A manager either issues the\nreceipt on the amount received (lines are re-apportioned) or refunds.\n\n## Webhooks\n\n`POST /api/webhooks/razorpay/{code}` and `/api/webhooks/stripe/{code}`. The `{code}` is an unguessable\nper-organisation value shown in settings.\n\n- The signature is checked with the organisation's webhook secret (Razorpay: HMAC of the body; Stripe:\n  `t.v1` with a five-minute tolerance).\n- `ProcessedWebhookEvent (provider, eventId)` makes every event apply once.\n- Business outcomes always answer **200**; unknown events are acknowledged; only infrastructure errors give 5xx,\n  so the provider retries. Razorpay disables endpoints that keep failing, so this matters.\n- Events used: Razorpay `payment.captured`, `refund.processed`, `refund.failed` (`payment.failed` is\n  acknowledged but closes nothing: one order takes several attempts, so a member can retry after a failure). Stripe\n  `checkout.session.completed` (when `payment_status=paid`), `checkout.session.async_payment_succeeded`,\n  `checkout.session.async_payment_failed`, `checkout.session.expired`, `refund.created`, `refund.updated`,\n  `refund.failed`.\n\n## Reconcile\n\nThe scheduler polls open gateway attempts older than five minutes, every five minutes, through\n`fetchPayment`, and settles or fails them. A member returning from Stripe, or finishing Razorpay Checkout,\nsettles straight away the same way. A Razorpay order whose attempts all failed stays open, since it can still\nbe paid. Attempts still open after a week are closed as `FAILED`; a capture that still arrives is receipted and\nflagged. The gateway can't be switched while the current one has attempts open from the last week, because\nwebhooks and polling only reach the saved provider.\n\n## Offline payments and maker-checker\n\nWith **\"A second person confirms offline payments\"** on (Settings → Money and year), a payment recorded by one\nowner, manager or treasurer counts only after a different one confirms it. The receipt number is allocated at\nconfirmation. With it off, recorded payments are paid and receipted at once.\n\nMembers can say \"I've paid\" with a UPI or bank UTR. A reference can be claimed once per organisation (a\npartial unique index ignores rejected claims, so a corrected claim can be resubmitted). Claims are rate\nlimited per member.\n\n**Instructions per unit.** A unit can say how its members pay (`OrgUnit.paymentInstructions`, the unit page,\n`setUnitPaymentInstructions`). Members see the nearest non-empty instructions, from their own unit up, then the\norganisation's `offlineInstructions` (`lib/payments/unitInstructions.ts`, loaded by `lib/payments/payGuidance.ts`).\nThey appear on the pay page and pay links, the join confirmation, the payment emails and, without a gateway, renewal\nreminders. With no gateway (`provider` NONE or no settings row) the wording reads \"Pay your fee to your unit\".\n\n**Collection sheet** (`/dashboard/[org]/payments/collect`, `recordCollection`). Staff with `payments.manage` pick a\nunit within their reach, tick members who owe, and record method, day received and each paper receipt-book number\n(`reference`), up to 200 rows at a time. Each row is its own transaction through `recordOfflinePayment`, so the lock\norder holds and one failure never blocks another; each row reports recorded, already paid or failed. The day received\ngoes in the note: `paidAt` stays the recording time, so receipts stay in order and memberships start as usual.\nStaff held for one unit reach only members in its subtree (`scopedTerm`, `memberWhere`).\n\n## Refunds and credit notes\n\nRefunds are append-only. Offline refunds are recorded as money already handed back and processed at once.\nGateway refunds go to the provider after the refund row commits and stay `PENDING` until the provider (or its\nwebhook) confirms; a timeout keeps them pending rather than guessing. Each processed refund gets a\n**credit note** with tax apportioned pro rata.\n\nA manager may tick **Also end the membership** when refunding: the term the payment bought is cancelled\n(`withdraw`, so it shows as cancelled, not revoked) and its card stops verifying.\n\n### Refund policy and member requests\n\nSettings → Payments and tax → **Refunds** sets the policy (`OrgPaymentSettings.refundPolicy`):\n\n- **No refunds** (the default): members can still leave, but nothing is offered.\n- **Full refund within N days** of payment: everything still refundable.\n- **Unused share within N days**: the membership-fee lines (tax included) times the unused share of the term;\n  joining and rejoining fees are never offered. A lifetime term offers its whole membership fee. The maths is\n  `lib/payments/refundPolicy.ts` (pure, tested).\n\nA member with a paid, running membership inside the window sees **Cancel and request a refund** on their\nmembership page, with the amount on offer and the policy text. That opens a `RefundRequest` (one open request\nper payment, enforced by a partial unique index) and emails the organisation-wide finance team. Nothing changes\nuntil a manager decides on the payment's page (or the **Refund requests** filter on Payments):\n\n- **Approve**: the amount is prefilled from the policy and may be changed up to what is left; the refund runs\n  as above and, unless unticked, the membership ends. The member is emailed.\n- **Decline**: needs a note, which is emailed to the member; the membership carries on.\n- The member may **withdraw** an open request. A refund made straight from the payment page closes any open\n  request on it.\n\nRequests are never deleted and are decided once (trigger in migration 0010). Erasure keeps them as money history\nbut replaces the member's reason; the member's data export includes them.\n\n### Pending refund follow-up\n\nEach scheduler pass (at most every five minutes) asks Razorpay or Stripe about gateway refunds still `PENDING`\nafter ten minutes, oldest-checked first, for up to 30 days: by the gateway refund id, or, when the original call\ntimed out before answering, by our refund id among the payment's refunds (`notes.refundId` /\n`metadata.refundId`). Only the provider's answer settles or fails a refund. Refunds pending for more than a day\nare flagged on the Payments page.\n\n## Immutability\n\nDatabase triggers enforce, with column allowlists:\n\n- a paid or waived payment may only gain settlement details, review flags or anonymisation; it is never\n  deleted;\n- payment lines are written once (lines of an unpaid payment may be deleted with it);\n- a tax document may only gain `cancelledAt`; never deleted;\n- a refund may only move on from `PENDING`.\n\nAn organisation that has taken payments cannot be deleted; a platform admin archives it instead.\n\n## Documents\n\nNumbers are gapless per organisation, series and financial year: `PREFIX-2627-00042` for receipts and invoices,\n`PREFIX-2627-C00042` for credit notes (at most 16 characters). PDFs are drawn on demand from the frozen\nsnapshot (`/api/documents/{id}`, for the finance team within their unit, or the member it was issued to), so\na rendering problem never burns a number. Amounts are printed with the ISO code (\"INR 1,180.00\").\nReceipts are attached to the payment email." },
  { slug: "gst", section: "operators", title: "Tax on membership payments", summary: "How Space Works Organization taxes payments and which document it issues. This follows the GST rules as commonly applied to membership fees, but it is not tax advice. Check your…", body: "# Tax on membership payments\n\nHow Space Works Organization taxes payments and which document it issues. This follows the GST rules as\ncommonly applied to membership fees, but **it is not tax advice. Check your setup with your accountant.**\n\n## Tax modes\n\nSet per organisation under **Settings → Payments and tax**. A payment freezes the settings it was created with\n(`taxSnapshot`), so changing them never alters earlier receipts.\n\n| Mode | Use it when | Document |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| No tax | Not registered for any tax | Receipt (\"This is a receipt, not a tax invoice.\") |\n| One tax rate | Outside India, e.g. VAT; any currency | Receipt showing the named tax |\n| Indian GST | GST-registered; INR plans | See below |\n\n### Indian GST\n\n| Situation | Document | Tax |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Regular scheme, taxable | Tax invoice (issued at receipt, s.31(2)) | The chosen rate, default 18% |\n| Composition scheme, or membership exempt | Bill of supply (Rule 49) | None |\n| Plan in another currency, \"export under LUT\" | Tax invoice, zero-rated, with the LUT note | 0% |\n| Plan in another currency, \"no GST\" | Receipt | None |\n\nSAC defaults to **999599** (services provided by other membership organisations). The GSTIN's first two digits\nmust match the registered state.\n\n## The maths\n\nAll amounts are integer minor units (paise for INR).\n\n1. **Inclusive pricing** (the default): the plan price is the total. Taxable value =\n   `round(price × 10000 / (10000 + rateBps))`; tax = price − taxable.\n2. **Exclusive pricing**: taxable value = price; tax = `round(price × rateBps / 10000)`.\n3. **Place of supply** is the member's state, falling back to the supplier's state\n   (IGST Act s.12(2)(b)).\n4. **Same state** → CGST and SGST, two equal halves. If the tax is an odd number of paise, the extra paisa\n   moves into the taxable value, so the halves match and the total never changes.\n5. **Different state** → IGST, the whole tax.\n6. The term fee and the joining (or rejoining) fee are separate lines, each taxed on its own.\n7. **Credit notes** (refunds) share the refund across the original lines by amount, then each line's share\n   across its taxable value and taxes, largest remainder first, so every column adds up exactly. A full refund\n   reverses the invoice exactly.\n8. A **mismatched gateway amount**, once accepted by a manager, is re-apportioned the same way.\n\nThe functions are `resolveTaxSnapshot`, `splitTax`, `chargeLines`, `apportion` and `creditNoteLines` in\n`lib/payments/tax.ts`, covered by `tests/unit/payments-tax.test.ts`.\n\n## Numbering\n\nOne gapless series per organisation per financial year (April–March by default, in the organisation's\ntimezone) for receipts, tax invoices and bills of supply, and a second series for credit notes. Numbers are at\nmost 16 characters (Rule 46): an optional prefix of up to four letters or digits, the year, then a five-digit\nsequence (`KUNJ-2627-00042`, `KUNJ-2627-C00007`). Allocation uses\n`INSERT … ON CONFLICT … DO UPDATE … RETURNING` in the same transaction as the payment, so the first document of\na year needs no setup and concurrent payments never skip or repeat a number.\n\n## What the documents show\n\nSupplier name, legal name, address, state and GSTIN; recipient name, member number, state and GSTIN when known;\nplace of supply; SAC; each line's taxable value, CGST and SGST (or IGST, or the named tax) and amount; totals;\nhow and when it was paid; the membership period; and the notes the kind requires (LUT, bill of supply,\n\"computer-generated, no signature\").\n\nGSTR-1 and GSTR-3B style summaries are in the finance reports (see [REPORTS.md](REPORTS.md))." },
  { slug: "notifications-operators", section: "operators", title: "Email and chat notifications", summary: "Two kinds of email go out, through two routes.", body: "# Email and chat notifications\n\n## Email\n\nTwo kinds of email go out, through two routes.\n\n| Kind | Examples | Sent through |\n|---|---|---|\n| Account and team emails | password reset, email verification, sign-in codes, new-device alerts, owner invites, organisation review notices, monthly report digest, members waiting to be verified | the platform's email provider (Super admin → Settings, or `EMAIL_PROVIDER` and its variables) |\n| Member emails | membership notices and reminders, receipts and credit notes, member invites, ID cards, refund and erasure replies | the organisation's own provider when set up and on; otherwise the platform's |\n\n### Email providers\n\nEvery install supports the same four providers, for the platform and for each organisation that brings its own:\n**SMTP**, **Amazon SES**, **Resend** and **Postmark**. Email is off until one is fully set up; a basic install\nworks without it (nothing is sent and the operations page shows email as unavailable).\n\n| Provider | Fields | Idempotency | Bounce and complaint reports |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| SMTP | host, port, login, password (Nodemailer; TLS required in production) | none | none |\n| Amazon SES | region, access key ID, secret access key (and optional session token from the environment); SES v2 `SendEmail` signed with SigV4 as a raw message | none: the outbox claim de-duplicates | SNS signature, from the one pinned topic |\n| Resend | API key (REST) | `Idempotency-Key` = the outbox id | Svix signature |\n| Postmark | server token and message stream (REST) | none: the outbox id travels as metadata and the claim de-duplicates | basic auth with the saved secret |\n\nThe HTTPS providers are reached only at their own fixed hosts over TLS, never through redirects, with a 20 second\ntimeout; the SES host is built from a validated region. Secrets are encrypted at rest (the platform's with\n`MFA_ENCRYPTION_KEY`'s derived key, an organisation's with `PAYMENT_SECRETS_KEY`'s email key), write-only (a saved\nsecret is never sent back to the browser, only that one exists; leaving the field blank keeps it) and never put in\nlogs, audit rows, URLs or action replies. Changes are audited by field name only. Platform settings:\nSuper admin → Settings → Email provider. An organisation's own: Settings → Email (owners and managers).\n\n**Send a test** sends to the person testing, through what is on the form (saved secrets fill blank fields), before\nanything is saved; ten an hour. A test of exactly what is saved is kept as the last test that went through, and the\nSuper admin → Email page shows the provider as unavailable when it is not set up or its last test failed.\n\n**Suppression.** Bounces and complaints reported by a provider go on one suppression list (`EmailSuppression`:\naddress lower-cased, reason `BOUNCE` or `COMPLAINT`, provider). Only permanent bounces and complaints are added.\nThe platform has one list; an organisation with its own provider has its own (`organisationId`). Every send\nchecks its account's list: a suppressed address is not sent to, and the outbox row fails at once with that reason\n(no retries). Webhooks: `POST /api/email/webhooks/{ses|resend|postmark}` (add `?org=<organisation id>` for an\norganisation's own provider; the settings screen shows the exact URL). Nothing in the body is read until the request\nis verified: Resend by its Svix signature (HMAC-SHA256 of id, timestamp and body, five-minute tolerance), Postmark by\nbasic auth whose password is the saved webhook secret, SES by the Amazon SNS signature (certificate only from an\n`sns.<region>.amazonaws.com` HTTPS address, the topic ARN pinned in settings, an hour's tolerance; a subscription\nconfirmation is followed only after that). Unverified requests get 401 and write nothing.\n\n**Delivery guarantees.** Email goes through the outbox after the change commits. A row is claimed atomically and\nmarked sent as soon as the provider accepts, and never offered again. Resend's idempotency key makes a retry after\na crash safe; for SES, Postmark and SMTP the claim is the only de-duplication, so a crash or a lost reply between\nacceptance and the database write can send one message twice. Rejected recipients and suppressed addresses fail\nwithout retries; outages and throttling retry with backoff.\n\n**Not used here:** push (FCM, APNs) and AI (Anthropic). This product has no native apps to push to and no AI\nfeature, so neither provider is configured; they are listed in the product rules for the products that do.\n\n**An organisation's own email** lives in Settings → Email (owners and managers): sender name and address,\nreply-to, and the provider above (for SMTP: host, port 587 or 2525 STARTTLS, 465 TLS, 25, login and password).\n**Send test email** records when it last went through. In production TLS is required and an SMTP host must\nresolve to public addresses.\n\nWithout its own settings an organisation's member emails come from the platform address but in the\norganisation's name (`\"Kunj Company\" <noreply@platform>`), with replies going to its contact email.\n\nCode: `sendBrandedEmail({ organisationId, … })` in `lib/email/message.ts` picks the route through\n`organisationEmailTransport` (`lib/email/orgTransport.ts`); emails without `organisationId` use the platform\n(`getEmailTransport`, `lib/email/transporter.ts`). Both return one `Mailer` interface (`lib/email/providers/types.ts`):\n`resend.ts`, `postmark.ts`, `ses.ts` with `sigv4.ts`, or Nodemailer for SMTP, wrapped by `withSuppression`\n(`lib/email/suppression.ts`). Fields and secrets: `lib/email/providerConfig.ts`. Webhooks: `lib/email/webhooks/*`.\n\n### Delivery\n\nEvery email and chat alert is a row in the database outbox (`enqueueOutbox` in `lib/outbox.ts`, kinds in\n`lib/outboxKinds.ts`), queued in the same transaction as the change it reports, so a rolled-back change never\nsends anything. PostgreSQL is the source of truth; Redis only ever carries row ids.\n\n- The BullMQ `beat` runs the `outbox-relay` job every minute (`lib/queue/registry.ts`). It hands the 100 oldest\n  due rows, and any claim left stale for 15 minutes, to the `outbox` queue by id.\n- The `worker` delivers those rows one id at a time, `QUEUE_OUTBOX_CONCURRENCY` (5) at once per worker. Each row\n  is claimed atomically (`PENDING` → `PROCESSING`), so several workers never send one twice.\n- A failed send goes back to `PENDING` with exponential backoff (2, 4, 8… minutes, at most an hour; at most\n  8 minutes for one-hour links), and the queued job waits until the row is due again. After 8 attempts, or a\n  payload that can never be sent, the row is `FAILED` and shown in Super admin → Operations.\n- Rows that carry a bearer link or code (password resets, verification, invites, sign-in codes) are deleted once\n  sent, and short-lived ones are dropped rather than sent after they expire.\n- The operations page's \"Outbox scheduler\" heartbeat is fresh while the relay runs; it shows the last delivery's\n  counts and error.\n- Hosts without a beat call `POST /api/internal/outbox` once a minute with `Authorization: Bearer $CRON_SECRET`;\n  a worker still sends. Development without Redis can set `JOBS_INPROCESS=true` to send from the web process.\n\n### Wording of member emails\n\nSettings → Email → **Wording of member emails** lets an organisation write two member emails in its own words:\n\n| Kind | Email |\n|---|---|\n| `card-issued` | the membership card (PDF attached): sent when a card is first issued on activation, and by **Email card** |\n| `membership-activated` | the notice that a membership has started |\n\nEach has a subject, a heading and a body (`OrgEmailTemplate`, one row per organisation and kind; subject and\nheading up to 150 characters, body up to 5,000). The body is plain text: a blank line starts a new paragraph, and\nevery paragraph is escaped by `sendBrandedEmail`, so nothing typed is ever sent as HTML. Placeholders are `{name}`\n(the member's full name), `{organisation}`, `{plan}`, `{unit}`, `{cardNumber}` and `{validUntil}`; one with no value\n(a member without a unit) is left empty, and anything else in curly brackets is sent as typed.\n\nThe editor shows a live preview with sample details. **Send me a test** sends the wording in the editor to the\neditor's own verified address through the organisation's email route (ten an hour). **Use the standard wording**\ndeletes the row. Saving needs `org.settings` held for the whole organisation and is audited\n(`ORG_EMAIL_TEMPLATE_SAVED`, `ORG_EMAIL_TEMPLATE_RESET`). Without a row the standard wording is used.\n\nCode: `lib/email/templates.ts` (pure rendering, shared with the preview), `lib/email/orgTemplates.ts`,\n`app/actions/email-templates.ts`.\n\n### Card email on activation\n\nWhen a membership's activation issues a member's first card on a plan (`issueCardOnActivation`), a `member-card`\nemail is queued in the same transaction, if the member has an email address and the organisation is active. The row\nholds only ids; the PDF is drawn when the email is sent and never stored. Renewals and reinstatements that keep the\ncard send nothing, so one activation sends at most one card email. Queuing it starts the 10-minute cooldown on\n**Email card**.\n\n### Sheet onboarding and unit verification\n\nAn import (CSV or a shared Google Sheet) can **Send to the unit's lead to verify**: each imported person gets a\nmembership on the chosen plan waiting for approval (`openTerm` with `needsVerification`, status event\n`sent-to-verify`), even though staff imported them. These people never applied, so no \"application received\"\nemail or chat alert goes out for them. Instead, once the import finishes, staff holding `members.manage` whose\nreach takes in at least one of them (their role held for the whole organisation, or for the member's unit or an\nancestor) get **one** `verification-alert` email for the import, with how many are waiting for them and a link to\nMembers → Waiting for approval. The importer is never emailed about their own import. The alerts are queued in the\nimport's closing transaction, with its audit row (`lib/membership/importAlerts.ts`, `lib/email/verificationAlert.ts`).\n\nVerifying (approving) starts the membership, issues the card and queues the card email as above. A lead can verify\nonly members in their own subtree.\n\n## Text messages (SMS)\n\nPhone sign-in codes are the only text messages the platform sends. The operator picks **Twilio** or **MSG91** under\nSuper admin → Auth → SMS (or `SMS_PROVIDER` and its variables in `docs/CONFIGURATION.md`). Off until set up; without\none, phone sign-in is unavailable and hidden. Everything else follows the shared outside-service contract:\n\n- **Secrets** (auth token, API key secret, MSG91 auth key, report token) are write-only and encrypted at rest; a saved one\n  is shown only as \"Saved\". Changes are audited by field name (`INSTANCE_SMS_PROVIDER_UPDATED`).\n- **Send a test** texts a number the administrator types, through what is on the form, before anything is saved. The\n  number is used once and never stored or audited; the result (`INSTANCE_SMS_TEST_SENT`, provider and outcome only)\n  is kept as the provider's health.\n- **Hosts.** The server connects only to `api.twilio.com` and `control.msg91.com`, over HTTPS, without following\n  redirects.\n- **Sending.** A code is queued as an outbox row of kind `sms-code` in the same transaction as its record, and sent by\n  the worker after commit. The outbox id is Twilio's idempotency token. Once the provider accepts, its message id is\n  stored on the code's record and a later delivery of the same row sends nothing, so a crash never sends twice. A refused\n  number or bad credentials fail for good; network errors, 429 and 5xx retry while the code lasts (ten minutes).\n- **Content.** The text carries the code and the product name only: \"123456 is your Space Works Organization code.\"\n  MSG91 sends the operator's approved DLT template, which takes the variables `otp` and `brand`.\n- **Delivery reports.** Twilio's status callback (`/api/sms/webhooks/twilio`) is requested on each message and checked\n  by its `X-Twilio-Signature` (HMAC-SHA1 over the address and the sorted fields, keyed with the account auth token), so it\n  needs the auth token. MSG91 has no signature: give it `/api/sms/webhooks/msg91?token=<report token>`. Reports update the\n  state (queued, sent, delivered, undelivered, failed) and the provider's error code on the code's record only; no number\n  or text is stored in them or in the audit log. Records are removed after two days.\n- **Unavailable, honestly.** With no provider, or one missing a field, the SMS panel says \"Unavailable\" and the sign-in\n  switch for phone codes shows \"Not configured\".\n\n## Chat alerts\n\nSettings → Notifications (owners and managers) holds up to ten chat channels per organisation. Each picks\nits alerts:\n\n| Alert | When |\n|---|---|\n| New application to review | someone applies to a plan that needs approval |\n| Payment received | a gateway or offline payment settles |\n| Payment waiting to be confirmed | a member submits a UPI or bank reference, or a recorded payment needs a second person |\n| Member asked for a refund | a refund request is opened |\n| Member asked to erase their details | an erasure request is opened |\n| Member left | a member leaves from their account |\n| Monthly summary | the first `monthly-digest` run of each month (hourly): active members, joined, lapsed, waiting |\n\nAlerts are team-only and short: what happened, member number, plan, amount, and a link to the dashboard.\nMember names appear only when the channel turns on **Show member names**; erasure requests never carry a name.\nAlerts are queued in the same transaction as the event and delivered by the worker with retries. A channel the\nprovider no longer accepts (deleted webhook, removed bot, revoked token) is switched off and its error shown.\n\n### Setting up each service\n\n- **Slack:** add the *Incoming Webhooks* app to a channel and paste its URL (`https://hooks.slack.com/services/…`).\n- **Discord:** channel settings → Integrations → Webhooks → New webhook → *Copy webhook URL*\n  (`https://discord.com/api/webhooks/…`). Mentions are disabled in every alert.\n- **Mattermost:** Integrations → Incoming webhooks → Add, then paste the URL (`https://your-server/hooks/…`).\n  The server must be reachable over HTTPS at a public address.\n- **Telegram:** create a bot with @BotFather, add it to your group or channel, and use the bot token and the\n  chat's numeric ID (or `@channelname` for a public channel).\n- **Signed webhook:** your own HTTPS address on a public host (checked when saving and again on every send, no\n  redirects). Each alert is posted as `{\"text\": \"…\"}` with `x-spaceworks-timestamp` (seconds) and\n  `x-spaceworks-signature` (`sha256=` and the hex HMAC-SHA256 of `timestamp.body` with your secret). Check the\n  signature and refuse timestamps more than a few minutes old. The address and secret are stored encrypted and\n  never shown again; **Send a test** is on each channel.\n- **WhatsApp:** uses the WhatsApp Business Cloud API (Meta). In WhatsApp Manager create a *Utility* template\n  named `team_alert`, language English (`en`), whose body is exactly `{{1}}`, and get it approved. Then enter the\n  phone number ID, a permanent access token (a system user token), and up to five recipient numbers with the\n  country code. Business-started WhatsApp messages can only use approved templates; each alert is sent as that\n  template's one parameter, on a single line.\n\nDestinations are stored encrypted (`PAYMENT_SECRETS_KEY`, a key derived for chat). Slack, Discord, Telegram and\nWhatsApp only ever post to their own provider's address; nothing follows redirects.\n\nCode: `lib/chat/*` (`events.ts`, `config.ts`, `send.ts`, `message.ts`, `notify.ts`); `queueChatAlerts` is called\nwhere each event happens." },
  { slug: "reports", section: "operators", title: "Reports", summary: "How Space Works Organization counts members and money. Every figure on every report follows these definitions. They are unit-tested: see tests/unit/reports-.test.ts and…", body: "# Reports\n\nHow Space Works Organization counts members and money. Every figure on every report follows these\ndefinitions. They are unit-tested: see `tests/unit/reports-*.test.ts` and `tests/integration/reports.db.test.ts`.\n\n## How it works\n\n- **Source of truth.** Membership metrics are computed from `MembershipTerm` dates and `TermStatusEvent.effectiveAt`, the domain time of each change. `recordedAt` is ignored, so a lapse the sweep records late still lapses on its grace end.\n  - The engine is pure: `lib/reports/dataset.ts` and `lib/reports/metrics/*`.\n  - `lib/reports/source.ts` loads the rows for one scope.\n- **Scope.** Every query is limited in three ways:\n  - to the organisation;\n  - to the viewer's unit subtree (`TeamMember.unitId`), narrowed further by the report's unit filter, which can never widen it;\n  - optionally to one plan.\n  A unit or plan id from another organisation is ignored. Cross-tenant URLs return 404.\n- **Periods.** A period is a half-open range of instants `[from, to)`.\n  - Its boundaries are local midnights in the organisation's timezone. FY 2026-27 in Asia/Kolkata runs from 31 Mar 2026 18:30 UTC to 31 Mar 2027 18:30 UTC.\n  - \"This financial year\" and the rolling presets stop at the end of today.\n  - The previous period, used for deltas, has the same length and ends where this one starts.\n- **Financial-year labels.** \"FY 2026-27\" for a year starting in April. A calendar-year organisation gets \"FY 2026\". Built on `lib/membership/periods.ts financialYearLabel`.\n- **Money.** Reports never add different currencies together.\n  - Every money figure is a list of per-currency totals, with the organisation's default currency first.\n  - Helpers: `lib/reports/money.ts` (`totals`, `groupTotals`, `subtractTotals`, `formatTotals`, `csvAmount`), built on `lib/money/currencies.ts` `totalsByCurrency`.\n  - A chart plots one currency (`format=\"money:INR\"`).\n- **Contact details.** Roles without `members.contact` (Viewer) see masked contact details everywhere, exports included: `s•••@e•••.com` and `•••• 45`.\n- **Drill-down.** Every tile and bar links to `/dashboard/[org]/members?drill=<metric>&period=…&plan=…&dunit=…`. The directory resolves the same member set through `lib/reports/drill.ts`, inside the viewer's own scope.\n- **Exports.** `GET /api/exports/reports/[org]/[report]` streams CSV and is rate-limited.\n  - When the stream ends it writes one audit row: `REPORT_EXPORTED`, or `FINANCE_REPORT_EXPORTED` for finance reports. The row holds the actor, report, period, plan, unit, `scoped`, `rows` and `complete`.\n  - An abandoned download is still audited, marked `complete: false`.\n\n## Membership definitions\n\nA term is **held** once it was activated (paid, approved or free): status `UPCOMING`, `ACTIVE`, `GRACE`, `ENDED`, `LAPSED` or `REVOKED`.\n\n- **Activation time.** The term's first event into `ACTIVE`/`UPCOMING` from `null`/`PENDING_*`/`EXPIRED`.\n- **Revocation time.** The last event into `REVOKED`.\n\n### Point-in-time standing\n\n| Metric | Definition |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Active(t)** | Distinct members with a held term where `startsAt ≤ t < endsAt` (lifetime terms have no end) and not revoked by `t`. **GRACE is not counted as active.** |\n| **Grace(t)** | Distinct members not active at `t` with a held term where `endsAt ≤ t < graceUntil`, not revoked by `t`. Reported separately. |\n| Closing instant | A period's closing standing is read just before `to`, and never later than now. |\n\n### Period metrics (period P = [from, to))\n\n**Membership flows**\n\n| Metric | Definition |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Joined (New)** | Members whose first activation, over all their terms in scope, falls in P. |\n| **Came back (Reactivated)** | Members not new in P with a `NEW`-kind term activated in P, after an earlier term of theirs had lapsed. Reinstating a revoked term is not a comeback. |\n| **Lapse instant** | `graceUntil` (or `endsAt` with no grace) of a held term with no held successor and no revocation. `ENDED` (renewed) terms never lapse. |\n| **Lapsed** | Members with a lapse instant in P, up to now. |\n| **Left (churned)** | Lapsed in P and neither active nor in grace at P's close. |\n| **Revoked** | Members with a revocation in P. Revoked terms are never due, renewed or lapsed. |\n\n**Renewals**\n\n| Metric | Definition |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Due** | Held, non-revoked terms whose `endsAt` falls in P and has arrived (at or before now). Counted per term, so one per member per plan. |\n| **Renewed** | Due terms with a held successor (`renewedFromId`), early renewals included. |\n| **Open** | Due terms that are neither renewed nor past grace. |\n| **Renewal rate** | Renewed ÷ (renewed + lapsed) among due terms. Open terms wait; the rate settles once grace ends. No decided terms gives \"—\", never 0%. This is Novi's \"Renewal %\"; some products divide by all due terms. |\n| **First-year renewal rate** | The same, restricted to the member's first held term on that plan. |\n\n**Rates**\n\n| Metric | Definition |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Churn rate** | Left ÷ Active(start of P). Nobody active at the start gives \"—\". |\n| **Retention rate** | (Active(end) − joined − came back) ÷ Active(start), capped at 100%. This is a headcount measure, unlike the renewal rate. |\n| **Net change / growth** | Active(end) − Active(start), and that change ÷ Active(start). |\n| **Bridge** | Start + joined + came back − left − revoked ± other = end. \"Other\" is grace timing (members in grace at either edge). |\n\n### Applications\n\n| Metric | Definition |\n| --- | --- |\n| Applied | A term's event into `PENDING_APPROVAL`. The decision is the next event out of it: approved (anything but `REJECTED`/`CANCELLED`), declined (`REJECTED`) or withdrawn (`CANCELLED`). |\n| **Median time to approve** | Median, and 90th percentile, of decision − application in hours, over approvals decided in P. Declines are not included. |\n| Approval rate | Approved ÷ (approved + declined), decided in P. |\n| Waiting | `PENDING_APPROVAL` now, whenever received, flagged after 7 days. |\n| **Join funnel** | `NEW` terms opened in P, followed to today: started → approved (or needed no approval) → activated → active now. Each step is a subset of the last, with drop-off per step. |\n\n### Breakdowns\n\n| Breakdown | Definition |\n| --- | --- |\n| Plan mix | Active members per plan at P's close. A member on two plans counts on both. |\n| Unit league table | One row per unit directly below the scope (or per top-level unit org-wide). Each row counts its whole subtree: active, grace, joined, lapsed, renewal rate and share of active. Members on the scope unit itself, or with no unit, get their own row. |\n| Member roles | Active members per role held. A member with no role is counted under \"No role\". |\n| Join source | New members in P by `Member.source` (join form, added by a manager, import). |\n| Geography | Active members by `stateCode`, or \"Not recorded\" when missing. |\n| Tenure | Active members by time since first activation (under 1, 1–2, 2–5, 5+ years), with the mean. |\n| Record gaps | Over all members in scope: no phone, no state (needed for GST place of supply), no unit, no photo (needed on cards), minors without guardian details. |\n| Expiry calendar | Active terms with no renewal, by the week they end, for the next 13 weeks. |\n| Win-back list | Lapsed in P and not active or in grace now. |\n\n### Trends and cohorts\n\n- **Trends** (`membershipTrend`):\n  - Stocks are read at each bucket's close.\n  - Flows are counted inside the bucket.\n  - Buckets are days for periods up to 45 days and months beyond.\n  - In the renewal-rate chart, months with fewer than 3 decided renewals are left out, so one lapse does not read as 0%.\n- **Cohort retention** (`cohortRetention`):\n  - A member's cohort is the local month of their first activation.\n  - Cell k is the share of the cohort active at the end of the k-th month after joining; month 0 is the joining month.\n  - Future cells are empty.\n\n### Daily rollup (`OrgDailyStats`)\n\n**Rows and fields**\n- The sweep (`runMembershipSweep` → `lib/reports/rollup.ts runStatsRollup`) writes one set of rows per organisation per **local day**.\n- There is one row per (unit, plan), plus an all-plans row (`planId` null) per unit with distinct members. A unit subtree sums its rows, and a plan filter picks that plan's rows.\n- Stocks (`active`, `grace`, `expired`) are taken at the end of the day; today's row is taken at now. `expired` is defined under \"Analytics\" below.\n- Flows:\n  - `joined`: new to the organisation on the all-plans row, new to the plan on a plan row.\n  - `reactivated`, `lapsed` and `applications`.\n  - `due`: terms ending that day.\n  - `renewed`: renewal terms activated that day.\n\n**Recomputation**\n- A day is recomputed whole: delete, then rewrite from source rows. Running it twice gives the same rows.\n- **Dirty days.** `markStatsDirty(client, organisationId, since)` marks every day from `since` to today. P7 calls it for backdated offline payments, late captures and refunds.\n- The sweep also marks days dirty itself when it finds status events recorded since the last run with an earlier `effectiveAt`.\n\n**Each run**\n- It rebuilds dirty days, yesterday (once the day ends) and today (every 15 minutes).\n- It backfills missing history up to 730 days, 120 days per organisation per run.\n- Heartbeat: `OperationalHeartbeat` key `report-rollup`.\n- **Expired backfill.** `runExpiredBackfill` (`lib/reports/rollupBackfill.ts`), called once per scheduler pass, rebuilds each organisation's whole calendar from the local day the backfill began back to its earliest stored day, including days with no rows: the old rollup wrote nothing for an all-zero day, which can now hold `expired` stock (after every membership lapsed). Up to 10 organisations and 60 days each per run, newest first. A day with nothing to count now keeps one zero all-plans row (so a month whose last members ended mid-month closes at zero rather than at the last non-zero day); progress is still kept per organisation in heartbeat `report-rollup-expired-backfill:<organisationId>` (`lastStartedAt` is the oldest day rebuilt; `lastSucceededAt` once the earliest stored day is reached). Runs walk the organisations by id from a cursor kept in the process, so an organisation that keeps failing is passed over and retried on the next full pass instead of holding up the rest. Heartbeat key `report-rollup-expired-backfill` gets `lastSucceededAt` when a pass from the start finds no organisation unfinished; the per-organisation rows are deleted then, and later runs stop after one read.\n\n**Readers**\n- The B1 trend reads the rollup (`lib/reports/rollupRead.ts`) when it covers the period and is fresh; otherwise it computes live, with identical stock definitions.\n- A2 and A3 read only the rollup.\n\n## Finance definitions\n\n| Metric | Definition |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Collected** | `PAID` payments by `paidAt` in P, **net of refunds by refund date**: `PROCESSED` refunds by `processedAt` in P, whenever the payment was. Waived payments are never collected. |\n| Revenue splits | By plan, unit, provider and method, each refund against its payment's group. By fee kind, a refund is apportioned over the payment's lines pro rata, by largest remainder, so parts add up exactly. |\n| Average per paying member | Net collected ÷ distinct members who paid in that currency in P. |\n| **Outstanding** | `PENDING` and `AWAITING_CONFIRMATION` payments, plus **`PENDING_PAYMENT` terms with no Payment row**. |\n| Ageing | Days past the due date: the term's `paymentDueBy`, else when the charge was raised. Bands: not yet due, 0–30, 31–60, 61–90, over 90. |\n| Collection rate | Of charges raised in P (payments created except cancelled, failed and waived, plus unpaid terms opened), the share by value now paid, per currency. |\n| Failed payment rate | Online (Razorpay, Stripe) payments created in P that `FAILED` ÷ those `PAID` or `FAILED`. |\n| Waivers | `WAIVED` payments created in P (count and forgiven amount); they carry no lines or document. |\n| Offline queue (B8) | `AWAITING_CONFIRMATION` claims, oldest first, and flagged payments. |\n| Maker-checker (B8) | Offline payments paid in P, split into confirmed by someone other than the recorder and self-confirmed. Also the median hours from claim to confirmation. |\n| Renewal forecast | Active terms ending in the next 30, 60 or 90 days with no renewal, at their plan's renewal price (\"at stake\"), and that × the trailing 12-month renewal rate (\"expected\"). |\n| Forecast vs actual | Per past month: the renewal value that fell due, against money collected for renewals of those terms. |\n| Documents register (B6) | Documents issued in P. The continuity check covers every series and financial year, whatever the filter: sequence 1…last with no gaps and no repeats; cancelled numbers are listed. |\n| GSTR-1 (B7) | One return month, INR, live documents only (see the table below). |\n| GSTR-3B | 3.1(a) outward taxable supplies net of credit notes; 3.1(c) nil-rated and exempt; 3.2 inter-state supplies to unregistered persons by place of supply. A monthly liability chart is shown over the period. |\n| SIMPLE tax | Documents with `otherTaxMinor`: taxable and tax per currency and rate, net of credit notes. |\n\nThe GSTR-1 tables in B7:\n\n| Table | Contents |\n| --- | --- |\n| 4A B2B | Members with a GSTIN. |\n| 5 B2C large | Inter-state, unregistered, invoice value **over ₹1 lakh**, per Rule 59(4) as amended by Notification 12/2024-CT from 1 Aug 2024. |\n| 7 B2C others | Everything else unregistered, by place of supply, rate and inter/intra, net of the credit notes against those supplies. |\n| 8 | Nil-rated and exempt (bills of supply). |\n| 9B | Credit notes: CDNR (registered) and CDNUR (against B2C large). Others are netted into Table 7. |\n| 12 | SAC summary split into B2B and B2C tabs (May 2025 layout), UQC `NA`, quantity 0. |\n| 13 | Documents issued per nature and series: from, to, total, cancelled, net. Mandatory from May 2025. |\n\nThe register is laid out like the returns, but the figures must be checked with a chartered accountant before filing.\n\n### Cash in hand\n\nOffline payments (`provider` OFFLINE, PAID or AWAITING_CONFIRMATION) recorded in the period (`createdAt`) by someone\nother than the member's own account, so member UTR claims and waivers are left out. Each is net of its refunds\n(pending or processed). Grouped per collector (`recordedById`) and per member's unit, per currency, with the part\nstill awaiting confirmation and the day the oldest was recorded. Scoped viewers see their subtree. Needs\n`reports.finance`; pure in `lib/reports/finance/cashInHand.ts`.\n\n## Reports catalogue\n\n### Platform (super-admin, `/super-admin/reports`)\n\n| Report | What it shows |\n| --- | --- |\n| A1 Tenant pipeline | Organisations by status, applications and decisions in the window, median time to approve, oldest waiting. |\n| A2 Tenant base | Organisations with active members per month end; largest organisations (rollup). |\n| A3 Members across tenants | Active and in grace across all organisations per month end (rollup). |\n| A4 Payment volume | Paid per currency, never converted, by provider, with organisation count. |\n| A5 Usage vs limits | Members and team against `memberLimit` and `managerLimit`, fullest first. |\n| A6 Outbox health | Messages by status, queued over 15 minutes, 14-day failure rate (details: Email). |\n| A7 System health | Scheduler and rollup heartbeats, database size, stored files; webhook backlog once P7 lands (details: Operations, Storage). |\n| Analytics (H7, `/super-admin/analytics`) | Every organisation compared (sortable), platform trends and failures by organisation; CSV export audited as `PLATFORM_REPORT_EXPORTED`. See \"Platform analytics\". |\n| Organisation analytics (H7, `/super-admin/orgs/[id]/analytics`) | One organisation's member trend, renewal rate, revenue per currency, top-level breakdown and limits used. Each view writes `SUPER_ADMIN_ORG_ANALYTICS_VIEWED`, throttled to once per 30 minutes. |\n| A8 Platform audit | Platform-admin actions. Opening an organisation's page writes `SUPER_ADMIN_ORG_VIEWED`, throttled to once per 30 minutes. |\n\n### Organisation (`/dashboard/[org]/reports`)\n\n**Membership tabs**\n\n| Tab | Reports |\n| --- | --- |\n| Overview (B1) | Membership change bridge; renewal rate, churn, joined and time-to-approve tiles with deltas; active trend (area); joined vs lapsed; renewal-rate trend; standing now; attention list. PDF snapshot at `/reports/print` (browser print to A4). |\n| Units (H6) | Level league: the units one level below the current one with drill-down. See \"Level league\". |\n| Members (B2) | Unit league table, plan mix, join source, roles, state, tenure, record gaps, and the member register (masked for Viewer). |\n| Renewals (B3, B4) | Due, renewed, renewal rate, first-year rate, churn and retention; renewal-rate trend; 13-week expiry calendar; renewal worklist with bulk reminder; cohort retention; win-back list. |\n| Applications (B10) | Waiting, received, time to approve (median and p90), approval rate, join funnel, received vs approved, approvals queue. |\n\n**Finance and team tabs**\n\n| Tab | Reports |\n| --- | --- |\n| Money (B5, B8, B9) | Collected, average per paying member, collection rate, failed online payments; monthly collected and refunded per currency; splits by plan, unit, fee, provider and method; renewal forecast and forecast vs actual; outstanding with ageing; offline queue, flagged, maker-checker, waivers, refunds pending. |\n| Tax and receipts (B6, B7) | Numbering check, documents register, GSTR-1 tables, GSTR-3B, monthly liability, SIMPLE tax. |\n| Team activity (B11, B12) | Actions per team member by category, actions over time, latest actions; card issuance once P6 provides it. |\n\n**Who sees which tab**\n- Money and Tax need `reports.finance` (Owner, Manager, Treasurer).\n- Team activity needs `audit.view` (Owner, Manager). A unit-scoped manager sees only the team inside their subtree.\n- Everything else needs `reports.membership`.\n\n**Filters and the bulk reminder**\n- Filters on every tab: period (this FY, last FY, 12 months, 90 days, 30 days, this month, custom), plan and unit subtree.\n- **Bulk reminder (B3).** `sendRenewalReminders({ organisationSlug, termIds })` needs `members.manage`. It queues the existing `renewal-reminder` outbox kind with a `ReminderLog` key `before-manual-<day>` or `after-manual-<day>`, so there is one manual reminder per membership per day.\n  - The server re-checks scope and renewability, and skips anything else.\n  - It is audited as `RENEWAL_REMINDERS_SENT`.\n\n**Web visits.** A separate page (Reports, Web visits) and its own CSV, described under \"Web visits\" below.\n\n**Exports.** Report keys: `register`, `worklist`, `lapsed`, `approvals`, `cohorts`, `units`, plus the finance exports `revenue`, `outstanding`, `documents` and `gstr1`.\n\n### Members (portal)\n\nThe member portal (`/account`) shows each person's memberships, history and payments from `lib/portal/*`; there\nis no separate report engine for it.\n\n## Finance data contract (P7 wires this)\n\nTypes are in `lib/reports/finance/types.ts` and must not be renamed. The pure functions in `lib/reports/finance/*` depend on them. All loaders live in **one file, `lib/reports/finance/source.ts`**, and return `[]` until wired.\n\n```ts\nloadPayments(scope: ReportScope): Promise<FinancePayment[]>\nloadPaymentLines(scope: ReportScope): Promise<FinancePaymentLine[]>\nloadRefunds(scope: ReportScope): Promise<FinanceRefund[]>\nloadTaxDocuments(scope: ReportScope): Promise<FinanceTaxDocument[]>\nloadUnpaidTerms(scope: ReportScope): Promise<UnpaidTerm[]>          // already works; add `payments: { none: {} }`\nloadFinanceRows(scope: ReportScope): Promise<FinanceRows>           // all five\nloadFinancePlans(organisationId: string): Promise<FinancePlan[]>    // already works\nloadPlatformPayments(window: { from: Date; to: Date }): Promise<Array<FinancePayment & { organisationId: string }>>\nexport const FINANCE_TABLES_READY = false                           // flip to true once wired\n\ntype ReportScope = { organisationId: string; unitPath: string | null; unitId: string | null; planId: string | null; scoped: boolean; memberId?: string }\n// Scope every query with: organisationId, member: { is: scopedMemberWhere(scope) } (handles unit subtree and memberId), and term.planId when planId is set.\n```\n\n| Type | Fields |\n| --- | --- |\n| `FinancePayment` | `id`, `memberId`, `termId \\| null`, `unitId \\| null` (via member), `planId \\| null` (via term), `status` (`PENDING`, `AWAITING_CONFIRMATION`, `PAID`, `WAIVED`, `FAILED`, `CANCELLED`), `provider` (`RAZORPAY`, `STRIPE`, `OFFLINE`), `method \\| null`, `currency`, `amountMinor`, `createdAt`, `paidAt \\| null`, `flagged`, `recordedById \\| null`, `confirmedById \\| null` |\n| `FinancePaymentLine` | `paymentId`, `kind` (`TERM_FEE`, `JOINING_FEE`, `REJOIN_FEE`), `currency`, `amountMinor`, `taxableMinor`, `cgstMinor`, `sgstMinor`, `igstMinor`, `otherTaxMinor` |\n| `FinanceRefund` | `id`, `paymentId`, `currency`, `amountMinor`, `status` (`PENDING`, `PROCESSED`, `FAILED`), `createdAt`, `processedAt \\| null` |\n| `FinanceTaxDocument` | `id`, `paymentId \\| null`, `refundId \\| null`, `kind` (`RECEIPT`, `TAX_INVOICE`, `BILL_OF_SUPPLY`, `CREDIT_NOTE`), `number`, **`series`**, **`sequence`**, `financialYear` (\"2026-27\"), `issuedAt`, `cancelledAt \\| null`, `currency`, `totalMinor`, `taxableMinor`, `cgstMinor`, `sgstMinor`, `igstMinor`, `otherTaxMinor`, **`rateBps`**, `placeOfSupply \\| null`, **`recipientGstin \\| null`**, `sac` |\n| `UnpaidTerm` | `termId`, `memberId`, `planId`, `unitId \\| null`, `currency`, `amountMinor` (price + joining fee), `createdAt`, `paymentDueBy \\| null` |\n\nThe four fields in bold were added to the contract P7 was given:\n- `series` and `sequence` come from `DocumentCounter` (series, value), for the gap check and Table 13.\n- `rateBps` comes from the frozen tax snapshot.\n- `recipientGstin` is the member's GSTIN on the snapshot, for B2B.\n\nOnce wired, P7 also calls `markStatsDirty` (`lib/reports/rollup.ts`) inside backdated payment, late-capture and refund transactions.\n\n## Extension points\n\n- **P7 finance.** Wire `lib/reports/finance/source.ts` as above. The Money and Tax tabs, finance exports and A4 then fill in with no other change.\n- **P6 cards (B11).** Fill `lib/reports/cards.ts loadCardIssuance(scope, window)`; the contract is in its comment. Team activity shows the card tile only when it returns data.\n\n## Sources\n\n- Novi AMS, Membership Change Summary (Added / Dropped / Renewed, Renewal % and Retained %): https://intercom.help/novi-ams/en/articles/4711435-membership-change-summary-report\n- i4a, retention rate calculation: https://i4a.com/blog/retention-rate-calculation/\n- Marketing General, \"Essential math for membership marketers\" (tenure is 1 ÷ lapse rate): https://www.marketinggeneral.com/knowledge-bank/tipster/essential-math-for-membership-marketers\n- Wild Apricot, payment reports (income by source, outstanding by days): https://www.wildapricot.com/features/online-payment-processing\n- MemberPress, reporting: https://memberpress.com/advancedreporting\n- Companies Act s.88, register of members: https://corporatelawreporter.com/companies_act/section-88-of-companies-act-2013-register-of-members-etc/\n- Income-tax Rule 17AA, books of account for trusts: https://www.incometaxindia.gov.in/w/rule-17aa\n- B2C large threshold of ₹1 lakh (Notification 12/2024-CT): https://help.tallysolutions.com/tally-prime/gstr-1/b2c-large-invoices-5a-5b/\n- GSTN advisory: Table 12 split into B2B and B2C, Table 13 mandatory from May 2025: https://tutorial.gst.gov.in/downloads/news/updated_advisory_hsn_table12_25042025.pdf\n- Not verified: Glue Up and Hivebrite formulas, and the Supreme Court status of s.7(1)(aa) after the Kerala High Court's IMA ruling (April 2025).\n\n## Added after the first release (owner, 2026-10-20)\n\n- **Subscription income by financial year** (Money tab): fees received in the year, plus advances received\n  earlier, less advances for later years, plus arrears paid after the year end, less earlier years' arrears,\n  equals fees earned in the year. Fees are earned day by day over the membership; joining fees and lifetime\n  memberships count when received. Taxable values only, net of processed refunds, per currency. The identity\n  holds to the paisa (`lib/reports/finance/subscriptionIncome.ts`, tested).\n- **GSTR-1 JSON** (Tax tab, organisation-wide finance roles only, audited): the month's B2B, B2CL, B2CS,\n  credit notes, nil/exempt, SAC summary (Table 12, B2B and B2C) and documents issued (Table 13, codes 1\n  and 5) in the GST offline tool's layout (`version` GST3.2.4, `hash`), rupees with two decimals,\n  dd-mm-yyyy dates and two-digit GST state codes; B2CL carries its place of supply on the group only\n  (`lib/reports/finance/gstr1Json.ts`). In October 2026 a month covering every section validated against\n  GSTN's \"Save GSTR1\" API schemas v4.1 (the shape the offline tool writes) and v5.0 (whose Table 13 uses a\n  newer `doc_det_new` layout that the offline tool does not write). Still open it in the current offline\n  tool before uploading.\n- **Cohort retention by financial year** (Renewals tab): the share of each joining year still active at the\n  end of that year and the four after it.\n- **Monthly summary email**: on the first scheduler pass of each month, every owner, manager and treasurer\n  gets last month's members, renewals, money (finance roles only) and waiting applications, for what they\n  can see. Each person switches it off on the organisation overview. One claim per organisation and month.\n\n- **Receipts and payments account** (Accounts, organisation-wide finance roles): one statement per currency\n  and financial year, cash basis, split into cash and bank. Receipts are membership fees, joining fees and\n  tax collected (from paid payments, by `paidAt`) plus recorded receipts; payments are processed refunds (by\n  `processedAt`) plus recorded expenses. Opening balance plus receipts less payments is the closing balance.\n  Offline payments taken in cash count as cash; everything else as bank. Expenses and other receipts are\n  `LedgerEntry` rows: never deleted, only voided once with a reason, guarded by a trigger in migration 0009.\n  Opening balances are entered per year and currency. The statement is pure (`lib/accounts/statement.ts`, tested).\n\n## Analytics (H6, H7, owner 2026-10-29)\n\n| Metric | Definition |\n| --- | --- |\n| **Expired(t)** | Distinct members not active and not in grace at `t`, holding no paid-for term that starts later (`UPCOMING`, activated by `t`), whose latest term that had started by `t` ended or lapsed rather than being revoked. On a plan row the latest term is that plan's, and a live term on any plan still rules the member out. Pure: `expiredAt` in `lib/reports/metrics/standing.ts`. |\n\n**Dashboard home (H6).** `lib/reports/homeData.ts`, inside the viewer's unit scope:\n- Active members at each month end and joined against lapsed for the last 12 months, from the rollup when it covers them (`rollupTrend`), else live (`membershipTrend`). Needs `reports.membership`.\n- Renewals due: `ACTIVE` terms ending from now to the end of the 30th local day with no live renewal, by week of local days.\n- Collected per month and currency (paid by `paidAt`, refunds by refund date) and outstanding now (open payments plus unpaid terms), from `loadCollections` and `loadOpenCharges` in `lib/reports/finance/source.ts`. Needs `reports.finance`. One chart per currency.\n\n**Level league (H6, Reports → Units).** `levelLeague` in `lib/reports/metrics/levels.ts`. One row per unit directly below the current one (the top level organisation-wide), each counting its whole subtree, plus \"This unit directly\" (or \"No unit\") when it has anything:\n- Active now, and growth: (active now − active 30 or 90 days ago) ÷ active then, \"—\" with nobody then.\n- Renewal rate in the selected period (the B3 definition), with the number of decided terms.\n- Collected in the period, net of refunds, and outstanding now, per currency, by the member's unit (finance roles only).\n- `?unit=` picks the level and only narrows the viewer's scope; staff limited to a unit start there. A row with units below drills down a level; a bottom-level row opens its active members.\n\n**Platform analytics (H7).** Aggregates only (owner decision 3): counts, unit and organisation names, money totals; never a member's name, email or phone. Pure in `lib/reports/platformAnalytics.ts`, loaded by `lib/reports/platformAnalyticsSource.ts` and `platformOrgAnalytics.ts`.\n\n| Column | Definition |\n| --- | --- |\n| Active, growth | All-plans rollup rows on the organisation's latest rolled-up day, against 30 and 90 days before that day. |\n| Renewal rate | Rollup flows over 12 months: renewals activated ÷ (renewals activated + lapses). This approximates the B3 rate without reading every organisation's terms. |\n| Revenue | Paid payments by `paidAt` less processed refunds by refund date over the last 12 months, per currency. Money columns sort by the platform's most-used currency. |\n| Outstanding | Open payments plus unpaid terms with no payment, now, per currency. |\n| Last staff sign-in | Latest known-device sign-in (`KnownDevice.lastSeenAt`) of the organisation's team, platform admins left out. |\n| Limits used | The fuller of members ÷ `memberLimit` and team ÷ `managerLimit`. |\n| Dormant | Created over 60 days ago, with no staff sign-in and no paid payment in the last 60 days. |\n\nPlatform trends: active members at each month end (A3), organisations going live (`CREATED` or `APPROVED`), applications (`SUBMITTED`) and declines (`REJECTED`) per month, revenue per currency per month, and failures by organisation over 30 days (`FAILED` payments, `PAYMENT_WEBHOOK_UNMATCHED` audit rows, `FAILED` outbox messages by `payload.organisationId`). Months are UTC calendar months.\n\n\n### Web visits (owner, 2026-11-19)\n\nCounts of views of public pages, kept by this site itself (product rules section 8.1). Always on, and no person is stored: no cookie, no address, no identifier. Pure in `lib/reports/visits.ts`; rows are `WebVisitDaily` (organisation or the platform when null, UTC day, approved route template, channel `web`, count).\n\n| Figure | Definition |\n| --- | --- |\n| Visits | Sum of `count` over the period for the organisation's rows. Days are UTC days, so a period in the organisation's timezone can differ by up to a day at its edges. |\n| Visits each day, each month | The same sum per UTC day, with a point (zero when quiet) for every day; periods over 92 days group by month. |\n| Visits by page | The sum per route template (home, public page, join form and so on), busiest first, with its share of all visits. Never a literal address. |\n\n- **Counting.** A same-origin beacon on public pages sends the route template to `POST /api/visits`, which ignores tokens and private routes, unknown or inactive organisations, obvious bots, and callers over the per-address limit. Pages under `/o/<slug>` count for that organisation; every other public page counts for the platform.\n- **Who sees it.** The report at `/dashboard/<org>/reports/visits` (and its CSV, `/api/visits/export/<org>`, audited as `REPORT_EXPORTED`) needs `reports.membership`. It is whole-organisation public information, not member data, so staff limited to a unit may read it; the plan and unit filters do not apply. The super admin sees the platform total and every organisation's total in Analytics, and one organisation under its analytics page. Aggregates only.\n- **Cache.** Read through `lib/visits/read.ts` with `analyticsCacheKey` (organisation-wide) or `platformCacheKey`, five minutes; counts are not money and nothing invalidates them early.\n\n## Not applicable\n\n- **Table 11 advances**: receipts are issued when money arrives, so no advance without a document arises." },
];

